window.PHOTOS=[{"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Djemaa_el_Fna.jpg/1280px-Djemaa_el_Fna.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 31.62583333, "lng": -7.98944444, "p": "Jemaa el-Fnaa, Marrakesh, Morocco", "h": "About this photo: Djemaa el Fna in Marrakech. October 2013\n\nJemaa el-Fnaa: Jemaa el-Fnaa , also Jemaa el-Fna, Djema el-Fna or Djemaa el-Fnaa, is a square and market place in the medina quarter of Marrakesh, Morocco. It remains the main square of Marrakesh, used by locals and tourists.\n\nThe wider area, Marrakesh: Marrakesh or Marrakech is the fourth-largest city in Morocco. It is one of the four imperial cities of Morocco and is the capital of the Marrakesh-Safi region. The city lies west of the foothills of the Atlas Mountains. The city was founded circa 1070 by Abu Bakr ibn Umar as the capital of the Almoravid dynasty.", "a": "Boris Macek", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djemaa_el_Fna.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Istana_Negara_KL_12_2007_019.jpg/1280px-Istana_Negara_KL_12_2007_019.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2007, "lat": 3.132487, "lng": 101.695703, "p": "Istana Negara, Jalan Istana, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia", "h": "About this photo: The Istana Negara , Kuala Lumpur .\n\nIstana Negara, Jalan Istana: The Royal Museum along Jalan Istana was the former National Palace and former residence of the Yang di-Pertuan Agong of Malaysia. It stands on a 13-acre site, located on a commanding position on the slope of a hill of Bukit Petaling overlooking the Klang River, along Jalan Syed Putra. It was replaced by the new national palace as the official residence of the king in 2011. In 2013, it was converted into the Royal Museum and referred as Old Istana Negara.\n\nThe wider area, Kuala Lumpur: Kuala Lumpur , officially the Federal Territory of Kuala Lumpur, is the capital city and a federal territory of Malaysia. It is the most populous city in the country, covering an area of 243 km2 with a population of 2,111,536 as of 2026. Greater Kuala Lumpur, which itself includes the Klang Valley, is an urban agglomeration of 8.81 million people as of 2024.", "a": "Gryffindor", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istana_Negara_KL_12_2007_019.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Drone_view_of_Kuril_Flyover_Highway_areas_in_Dhaka_City.jpg/1280px-Drone_view_of_Kuril_Flyover_Highway_areas_in_Dhaka_City.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2020, "lat": 23.8223, "lng": 90.4206, "p": "Kuril Flyover, Dhaka, Bangladesh", "h": "About this photo: Drone view of Kuril Flyover Highway areas in Dhaka City\n\nKuril Flyover: The Kuril Flyover is the third largest flyover in Bangladesh. It is located in Dhaka. Its foundation stone was laid on 2 May 2010. Finally the work started in March 2011. The flyover was opened for traffic on 4 August 2013.\n\nThe wider area, Dhaka: Dhaka , formerly spelled as Dacca, is the capital and largest city of Bangladesh. With an estimated population of 36.6 million, Dhaka is the second largest city by population in the world, and is widely considered to be the most densely populated built-up urban area in the world. Dhaka is an important cultural, economic, and scientific hub of Eastern South Asia.", "a": "IbnSina20", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drone_view_of_Kuril_Flyover_Highway_areas_in_Dhaka_City.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/House_of_wonders.jpg/1280px-House_of_wonders.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": -6.160914, "lng": 39.189591, "p": "House of Wonders, Kiponda, Tanzania", "h": "About this photo: The House of Wonders in Stone Town, Zanzibar\n\nHouse of Wonders: The House of Wonders or Palace of Wonders is a landmark building in Stone Town, Zanzibar. It is the largest and tallest building of Stone Town and occupies a prominent place facing the Forodhani Gardens on the old town's seafront in Mizingani Road. It is located between the Old Fort and the Palace Museum . It is one of six palaces built by Barghash bin Said, second Sultan of Zanzibar, and it is said to be located on the site of the 17th-century palace of Zanzibari queen Fatuma. The House of Wonders housed the Museum of History and Culture of Zanzibar and the Swahili Coast.", "a": "Moongateclimber", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_of_wonders.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Horacio_Coppola_-_Buenos_Aires_1936_-_Corrientes_desde_el_edificio_COMEGA_nocturna.jpg/1280px-Horacio_Coppola_-_Buenos_Aires_1936_-_Corrientes_desde_el_edificio_COMEGA_nocturna.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1936, "lat": -34.6034, "lng": -58.3732, "p": "Safico Building, Buenos Aires, Argentina", "h": "About this photo: Vista nocturna de la Avenida Corrientes hacia el oeste y edificio SAFICO desde el edificio COMEGA, Buenos Aires, en 1936.\n\nSafico Building: The Safico Building is a 90-metre rationalist and art-déco skyscraper with some Bauhaus elements, built out of reinforced concrete in Buenos Aires, Argentina in 1933. The construction was carried out by Swiss engineer Walter Möll and was completed in 10 months becoming one of the first skyscrapers of the city, only shorter than a few buildings in Buenos Aires, such as the Palacio Barolo. It contains both residential apartments and offices. Since the 1940s, important international media companies set their Argentine offices in the Safico Building, such as The Washington Post, the Financial Times, BBC and Reuters. Nobel Prize Laureate Pablo Neruda lived for a few months in the building when he worked as a diplomat. He wrote the second part to Residence on Earth while living there, as well as a few other poems.\n\nThe wider area, Buenos Aires: Buenos Aires, officially the Autonomous City of Buenos Aires, is the capital and largest city of Argentina. It is located on the southwest of the Río de la Plata. Buenos Aires is classified as an Alpha− global city, according to the GaWC 2024 ranking. The city proper has a population of 3.1 million and its urban area has a population of 16.7 million, making it the 21st most populous metropolitan area in the world.", "a": "Horacio Coppola", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horacio_Coppola_-_Buenos_Aires_1936_-_Corrientes_desde_el_edificio_COMEGA_nocturna.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Seoul_City_Hall_from_Plaza.jpg/1280px-Seoul_City_Hall_from_Plaza.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": 37.56640556, "lng": 126.97782222, "p": "Seoul City Hall, Seoul, South Korea", "h": "About this photo: Seoul City Hall, seen from Seoul Plaza.\n\nSeoul City Hall: Seoul City Hall is a governmental building for the Seoul Metropolitan Government in South Korea, in charge of the administrative affairs of Seoul. It is located in Taepyeongno, Jung District, at the heart of Seoul. It is connected to City Hall Station on Seoul Subway Line 1, with access to Seoul Subway Line 2 from the same station. In front of the current city hall is the old city hall building, now Seoul Metropolitan Library, and Seoul Plaza.\n\nThe wider area, Seoul: Seoul, officially Seoul Special Metropolitan City, is the capital and largest city of South Korea, and the largest city in the whole of Korea. The broader Seoul Metropolitan Area, encompassing Seoul, Gyeonggi Province and Incheon, emerged as the world's sixth largest metropolitan economy in 2022, behind New York, Tokyo, Los Angeles, Paris, and London, and hosts more than half of South Korea's population.", "a": "Tristan Surtel", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seoul_City_Hall_from_Plaza.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Photo_taken_of_closure_of_Bank_of_Van_Diemen%27s_Land%2C_3_August_1891.jpg/1280px-Photo_taken_of_closure_of_Bank_of_Van_Diemen%27s_Land%2C_3_August_1891.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1891, "lat": -42.88210556, "lng": 147.32862222, "p": "Bank of Van Diemen's Land, Hobart, Australia", "h": "About this photo: Photo taken of the Bank of Van Diemen's Land building once they closed their doors on 3 August 1891.\n\nBank of Van Diemen's Land: Founded in 1823, the Bank of Van Diemen's Land was the first financial institution to be established in Van Diemen's Land. Affectionally referred to as the old bank, the Bank of Van Diemen's Land traded for 68 years before becoming the first major bank failure in what would become the Australian banking crisis of 1893.\n\nThe wider area, Hobart: Hobart is the capital and most populous city of the island state of Tasmania, Australia. Located in Tasmania's south-east on the estuary of the River Derwent, it is the southernmost capital city in Australia.", "a": "Unknown author Unknown author", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_taken_of_closure_of_Bank_of_Van_Diemen%27s_Land,_3_August_1891.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Transformers_-_Victoria_Square_Adelaide_SA_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Transformers_-_Victoria_Square_Adelaide_SA_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2014, "lat": -34.9281, "lng": 138.5999, "p": "Victoria Square, Adelaide, Adelaide, Australia", "h": "About this photo: Transformers - Victoria Square Adelaide SA\n\nVictoria Square, Adelaide: Victoria Square / Tarntanyangga , is the central square of five public squares in the Adelaide city centre, South Australia. It is one of six squares designed by the founder of Adelaide, Colonel William Light, who was Surveyor-General at the time, in his 1837 plan of the City of Adelaide which spanned the River Torrens Valley, comprising the city centre and North Adelaide. The square was named on 23 May 1837 by the Street Naming Committee after Princess Victoria, then heir presumptive of the British throne. In 2002, it was assigned a second name, Tarndanyangga , in the Kaurna language of the original inhabitants, as part of the Adelaide City Council's dual naming initiative. The square has been upgraded and modified several times through its lifetime. It has become a tradition that during the Christmas period a 24.5-metre tall Christmas tree is erected in the northern part of the square.\n\nThe wider area, Adelaide: Adelaide is the capital and most populous city of South Australia, as well as the fifth-most populous city in Australia. The name \"Adelaide\" may refer to either Greater Adelaide or the Adelaide central business district; the demonym Adelaidean is used to denote the city and the residents of Adelaide.", "a": "Trentino Priori", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transformers_-_Victoria_Square_Adelaide_SA_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Vista_do_Orfi%C3%A3o_do_Porto.jpg/1280px-Vista_do_Orfi%C3%A3o_do_Porto.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": 41.14556111, "lng": -8.60694444, "p": "Cinema Batalha (Porto), Porto, Portugal", "h": "Cinema Batalha (Porto): The Cinema Batalha is a Portuguese cinema and concert venue in civil parish of Cedofeita, Santo Ildefonso, Sé, Miragaia, São Nicolau e Vitória, municipality of Porto. Originally known as the Salão High-Life , it was moved from Boavista by its owners to its current location, and rebuilt by architect Artur Andrade in the Art-Deco style, re-inaugurated on 3 June 1947. After many years of success, the building and the cinema began losing customers , and was closed in 2003. Re-opened as a cultural and concert venue in 2006, the reconstruction faithfully maintained the original Art-Deco style and is one of the more impressive examples of the movement in Porto and Portugal.\n\nThe wider area, Porto: Porto, also known in English as Oporto, is the second largest city in Portugal, after Lisbon. It is the capital of the Porto District and one of the Iberian Peninsula's major urban areas. Porto city proper, which is the entire municipality of Porto, is small compared to its metropolitan area, with an estimated population of 273,476 people in a municipal area of 41.42 km2 .", "a": "SoniaCardoso", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_do_Orfi%C3%A3o_do_Porto.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Melbourne_Mint_corner.jpg/1280px-Melbourne_Mint_corner.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": -37.812153, "lng": 144.956794, "p": "Melbourne Mint, Melbourne, Australia", "h": "About this photo: Melbourne Mint, William Street\n\nMelbourne Mint: The Melbourne Mint, located on the corner of William and La Trobe Streets in Melbourne, Victoria, Australia, was first established as a branch of the British Royal Mint, opening on 12 June 1872. The site operated as the Royal Melbourne Mint until 1968 and was subsequently repurposed as a history museum. The former mint was added to the Victorian Heritage Register on 12 October 1988 in recognition of its historical and architectural significance; and was, on an unknown date, added to the now defunct Register of the National Estate.\n\nThe wider area, Melbourne: Melbourne is the capital and most populous city of the Australian state of Victoria and the second-most populous city in Australia. The city's name generally refers to a 9,993-square-kilometre area, comprising an urban agglomeration of 30 local government areas.", "a": "Rohanstorey", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_Mint_corner.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Piccadilly_Gardens_2026.jpg/1280px-Piccadilly_Gardens_2026.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2026, "lat": 53.48032, "lng": -2.23751, "p": "Manchester Piccadilly Gardens bus station, Manchester, United Kingdom", "h": "About this photo: Piccadilly Gardens in 2026, post Bee Network\n\nManchester Piccadilly Gardens bus station: Manchester Piccadilly Gardens bus station, often abbreviated to Piccadilly Gardens, is one of two main bus stations serving Manchester city centre, Greater Manchester, England; the other is Shudehill Interchange.\n\nThe wider area, Manchester: Manchester is a city and metropolitan borough in North West England, at the centre of the eponymous county of Greater Manchester. It had a population of over 589,000 in 2024. It borders the Cheshire Plain to the south, the Pennines to the north and east, and the city of Salford to the west.", "a": "Hlfxcuc", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piccadilly_Gardens_2026.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/IMG_1088-01_Inya_Lake_view.jpg/1280px-IMG_1088-01_Inya_Lake_view.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2017, "lat": 16.82361111, "lng": 96.16277778, "p": "Yankin Township, Yangon City, Myanmar", "h": "About this photo: The image is so many views of inya lake.That is new one.\n\nYankin Township: Yankin Township is located in the north-central part of Yangon. The township comprises 15 wards, and shares borders with Mayangon township in the north, Thingangyun township and South Okkalapa township in the east, the Inya Lake, Bahan township and Mayangon township in the west, and Tamwe township in the south. The township has 13 primary schools, seven middle schools and two high schools. Yankin is a thriving commercial hub of Yangon. Yankin Centre is a leading shopping destination in Yangon, attracting shoppers from around the city. Saya San Road is the main thoroughfare that runs through most of the important places in the township. The Kokkine Swimming Pools, and the 18-hectare Myakyuntha Amusement Park are both located here. In 2008, the National Library of Myanmar moved to a new site in Yankin Township.", "a": "U Zaw Naing", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IMG_1088-01_Inya_Lake_view.jpg"}, {"u": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/4th_Avenue_Theater_color.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled", "y": 1985, "lat": 61.21833333, "lng": -149.89444444, "p": "Fourth Avenue Theatre (Anchorage, Alaska), Anchorage, United States", "h": "About this photo: Front elevation of the Fourth Street Theater, Anchorage, Alaska\n\nFourth Avenue Theatre (Anchorage, Alaska): The Fourth Avenue Theatre, also known as the Lathrop Building, was a movie theater in Anchorage, Alaska that has been described as Art Deco, Streamline Moderne, and Art Moderne in style. Built beginning in 1941 and completed in 1947 after a halt during World War II, somewhat after the heyday of these styles, it was a large 960-seat first-run theater until the 1980s. The theater was designed by B. Marcus Priteca, a leading designer of themed cinemas in America, in association with Seattle architect A.A. Porreca for Cap Lathrop, a prominent Alaska businessman. The theater's lobby featured a gold leaf mural of Mount McKinley, but originally omitted a concession stand, a feature Lathrop felt was inappropriate. The main house was decorated with silver and gold murals by Anthony Heinsbergen and Frank Bouman of Los Angeles, and a rendering of the Big Dipper on the ceiling.\n\nThe wider area, Anchorage, Alaska: Anchorage , officially the Municipality of Anchorage, is the most populous city in the U.S. state of Alaska. With a population of 291,247 at the 2020 census, it contains nearly 40 percent of the state's population. The Anchorage metropolitan area, which includes Anchorage and the neighboring Matanuska-Susitna Borough, had a population of 398,328 in 2020, accounting for more than half the state's population.", "a": "Jet Lowe", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:4th_Avenue_Theater_color.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Hong_Kong_street_in_daytime_featuring_a_myriad_of_overhead_signs%2C_1993.jpg/1280px-Hong_Kong_street_in_daytime_featuring_a_myriad_of_overhead_signs%2C_1993.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1993, "lat": 22.32195, "lng": 114.16944, "p": "Sai Yeung Choi Street, Hong Kong, China", "h": "About this photo: A Hong Kong street in the daytime featuring a myriad of overhead banners, with construction ditch in the middle, 1993.jpg Other information Photo by George Garrigues.\n\nSai Yeung Choi Street: Sai Yeung Choi Street are two streets in Mong Kok, Kowloon, Hong Kong, namely, Sai Yeung Choi Street South and Sai Yeung Choi Street North . Although officially two streets, local people seldom make distinction between them. They are separated by the Mong Kok Police Station. Sai Yeung Choi Street South is a popular hotspot for shopping and a tourist attraction.\n\nThe wider area, Hong Kong: Hong Kong is a special administrative region of China. Situated on China's southern coast just south of Shenzhen, it consists of Hong Kong Island, Kowloon, and the New Territories. With 7.5 million residents in a 1,114-square-kilometre territory, Hong Kong is the fourth-most densely populated region in the world.", "a": "GeorgeLouis", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hong_Kong_street_in_daytime_featuring_a_myriad_of_overhead_signs,_1993.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Rakovski_street_Sofia.jpg/1280px-Rakovski_street_Sofia.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": 42.69341389, "lng": 23.32781667, "p": "Georgi Rakovski Street, Sofia, Bulgaria", "h": "About this photo: Georgi Rakovski Street in Sofia, Bulgaria.\n\nGeorgi Rakovski Street: Georgi Rakovski Street , usually referred to by its old name of Rakovska, is a major street in Sofia, the capital of Bulgaria. Located in the center of the city between Slivnitsa Boulevard and Evlogi Georgiev Boulevard, it is named after the Bulgarian revolutionary Georgi Sava Rakovski. The street passes along some of Sofia's major landmarks, including the Alexander Nevsky Cathedral and the Central Military Club.\n\nThe wider area, Sofia: Sofia is the capital and largest city of Bulgaria. It is situated in the Sofia Valley at the foot of the Vitosha mountain, in the western part of the country. The city is built west of the Iskar river and has many mineral springs, such as the Sofia Central Mineral Baths. It has a humid continental climate. Known as Serdica in antiquity, Sofia has been an area of human habitation since at least 7000 BC.", "a": "MrPanyGoff", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rakovski_street_Sofia.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Gebrag_Khalil_Garden_%284694182661%29_cropped.jpg/1280px-Gebrag_Khalil_Garden_%284694182661%29_cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": 33.8942113, "lng": 35.5003362, "p": "Gibran Khalil Gibran Garden, Beirut, Lebanon", "h": "About this photo: Cropped version of File:Gebrag Khalil Garden .jpg\n\nGibran Khalil Gibran Garden: The Gibran Khalil Gibran Garden is a 6,000-square-meter public garden in the Centre Ville area of Beirut, Lebanon, facing the UN House, the headquarters of ESCWA, the United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Western Asia. The garden, which is named in honor of the Lebanese–American writer, poet and visual artist Kahlil Gibran, features two circular lawns, a fountain, and modern sculpture, including a bust of Gibran. The garden is often used as a venue for peaceful and democratic demonstrations and sit-ins.\n\nThe wider area, Beirut: Beirut is the capital and largest city of Lebanon. As of 2025, Greater Beirut has a population of 2.4 million, just under half of Lebanon's population, which makes it the twelfth-largest city in the Levant region and the sixteenth-largest in the Arab world. The city is situated on a peninsula at the midpoint of Lebanon's Mediterranean coast.", "a": "Gibranist", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebrag_Khalil_Garden_(4694182661)_cropped.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/%D9%85%D8%A8%D9%86%D9%89_%D9%85%D8%AD%D8%B7%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9_.jpg/1280px-%D9%85%D8%A8%D9%86%D9%89_%D9%85%D8%AD%D8%B7%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9_.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 33.32361111, "lng": 44.38027778, "p": "Baghdad Central Station, Baghdad, Iraq", "h": "About this photo: صورة خارجية حديثة توضح مبنى محطة القطار العالمية ببغداد\n\nBaghdad Central Station: Baghdad Central Station or Baghdad International Station is the main train station in Baghdad. It links the rail network to the south and the north of Iraq. The station was built by the British and designed by J. M. Wilson, a Scot who had been an assistant to Lutyens in New Delhi and who subsequently set up a practice of his own in Baghdad. It has been described as the \"Crown Jewel\" of Baghdad by the BBC. Construction started in 1948 and finished in 1953. The station is the biggest one in Iraq. It was renovated during the American occupation of Iraq in the 2000s.\n\nThe wider area, Baghdad: Baghdad is the capital and the largest city of Iraq. It is located on the banks of the Tigris in central Iraq. With an estimated population of 8 million, it ranks among the most populous and largest cities in the Middle East and the Arab world and constitutes 22% of Iraq's population. Baghdad is a primary financial and commercial center in the region.", "a": "Mondalawy", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%A8%D9%86%D9%89_%D9%85%D8%AD%D8%B7%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9_.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/La_bandera_en_la_Plaza_de_la_Democracia.JPG/1280px-La_bandera_en_la_Plaza_de_la_Democracia.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": -34.8936, "lng": -56.1642, "p": "Democracy Square, Montevideo, Uruguay", "h": "About this photo: This is a photo of a Uruguayan monument identified by the ID\n\nDemocracy Square: Democracy Square is a road junction and public space in the Tres Cruces neighbourhood of Montevideo, Uruguay. The square connects Italy Avenue, Artigas Boulevard and 8 de Octubre Avenue, which are three of the most important thoroughfares in the city, due to the large amount of traffic they receive as they are different entrance routes to Central Montevideo. Democracy Square, also known as Plaza de la Bandera due to the flagpole at its center, is a busy meeting place as it is located across from the Tres Cruces bus station and mall, the main bus terminal in the country.\n\nThe wider area, Montevideo: Montevideo is the capital and largest city of Uruguay. As of the 2023 census, the city proper has a population of 1,287,452, making up about 36.8% of the country's total population, in an area of 201 square kilometers . Montevideo is situated on the southern coast of the country, on the northeastern bank of the Río de la Plata.", "a": "Rosina Peixoto", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_bandera_en_la_Plaza_de_la_Democracia.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Luang_Prabang-Phou_Si-10-Santi_Chedi-gje.jpg/1280px-Luang_Prabang-Phou_Si-10-Santi_Chedi-gje.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2007, "lat": 19.88305556, "lng": 102.15027778, "p": "Wat Pa Phon Phao, Luang Prabang, Laos", "h": "About this photo: Phou Si mountain in Luang Prabang in Laos\n\nWat Pa Phon Phao: Wat Pa Phon Phao , also Wat Phon Phao, Wat Phonphao, or Wat Phol Phao, meaning \"Peacefulness Temple\", is a Buddhist temple situated on a hill in the southeast of Luang Prabang, Laos, across the Nam Khan River from Luang Prabang Airport. The temple, a golden stupa, is used as a forest meditation retreat, and was once headed by the abbot Ajahn Saisamut, a noted Lao Buddhist teacher. His funeral in 1992 was one of the largest funeral services Laos had ever seen. The temple has a fine collection of murals, some of them gruesome in nature.\n\nThe wider area, Luang Prabang: Luang Prabang , historically known as Xieng Thong, and alternatively spelled Luang Phabang or Louangphabang, is the capital of Luang Prabang Province in north-central Laos. Its name, meaning “Royal Buddha Image,” derives from the Phra Bang, a statue symbolizing Lao sovereignty.", "a": "Gerd Eichmann", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luang_Prabang-Phou_Si-10-Santi_Chedi-gje.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Lahore_Fort_view_from_Baradari.jpg/1280px-Lahore_Fort_view_from_Baradari.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": 31.54972222, "lng": 74.34361111, "p": "Lahore, Lahore District, Pakistan", "h": "About this photo: This is a photo of a monument in Pakistan identified as the\n\nLahore: Lahore is the capital and largest city of the Pakistani province of Punjab. It is the second-largest city in Pakistan, after Karachi, and 27th largest in the world, with a population of over 14 million. Lahore is one of the country's major industrial, educational and economic hubs, and is considered the historic capital and cultural centre of the wider Punjab region. The site of the city has been inhabited for over two millennia, although Lahore itself rose to prominence in the late 10th century. At times Lahore has served as the capital of several empires, including the Odi Shahi dynasty, Ghaznavid Empire and Delhi Sultanate. It was one of the principal cities in the Mughal Empire, being its capital city during the reign of Akbar. During this period, it was one of the largest cities in the world.\n\nThe wider area, Lahore District: Lahore District is a district within the Lahore Division of Punjab, Pakistan, consisting of the provincial capital of Lahore and surrounding areas. It is the most populous district of Pakistan, with a population of 12.9 million in 2023. The total area is 1,772 square kilometres .", "a": "Rohaan Bhatti", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahore_Fort_view_from_Baradari.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Zuccotti_Park_Spring_2015.JPG/1280px-Zuccotti_Park_Spring_2015.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2015, "lat": 40.709228, "lng": -74.011247, "p": "Zuccotti Park, New York, United States", "h": "About this photo: Zuccotti Park in the Financial District of New York City in Spring 2015\n\nZuccotti Park: Zuccotti Park is a 33,000-square-foot publicly accessible park in the Financial District of Lower Manhattan in New York City. It is located in a privately owned public space controlled by Brookfield Properties and Goldman Sachs. Zuccotti Park is bounded by Broadway to the east, Liberty Street to the north, Trinity Place to the west, and Cedar Street to the south. The park was created in 1968 by Pittsburgh-based United States Steel, after the property owners negotiated its creation with city officials. It was named Liberty Plaza Park because it was situated one block south of One Liberty Plaza. The park's northwest corner is across the street from Four World Trade Center. It has been popular with local tourists and financial workers. The park was heavily damaged in the September 11 attacks and subsequent recovery efforts of 2001.\n\nThe wider area, New York: New York most commonly refers to: New York City, the most populous city in the US, in the state of New York New York , in the northeastern US New York may also refer to:", "a": "MusikAnimal", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuccotti_Park_Spring_2015.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/00000-Rand_Club-Johannesburg-s.jpg/1280px-00000-Rand_Club-Johannesburg-s.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1988, "lat": -26.206063, "lng": 28.041309, "p": "Rand Club, Johannesburg, South Africa", "h": "About this photo: This media shows a South African Protected Site with SAHRA file reference 000000000 .\n\nRand Club: The Rand Club is a private members' club in Johannesburg, South Africa, founded in October 1887. The current clubhouse was designed by architects Leck & Emley in 1902 and its construction completed in 1904. Cecil John Rhodes helped to select the location. Reconceived in 2016, it changed policy to welcome members regardless of race or gender.\n\nThe wider area, Johannesburg: Johannesburg , colloquially known as Jozi, Joburg, or \"the City of Gold\", is the capital city of the province of Gauteng and the largest city in South Africa. In 2022 , the City of Johannesburg Metropolitan Municipality had a population of 4,803,262, and had a wider metropolitan area of 15,099,422, making it one of the 100 largest urban areas in the world.", "a": "Janek Szymanowski", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:00000-Rand_Club-Johannesburg-s.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Manaus_amazonas.jpg/1280px-Manaus_amazonas.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2022, "lat": -3.1189, "lng": -60.0217, "p": "Manaus, Brazil", "h": "Manaus: Manaus is the capital and largest city of the Brazilian state of Amazonas. It is the seventh-largest city in Brazil, with an estimated 2024 population of 2,279,686 distributed over a land area of about 11,401 km2 . Located at the east centre of the state, the city is the centre of the Manaus metropolitan area and the largest metropolitan area in the North Region of Brazil by urban landmass. It is situated near the confluence of the Negro and Amazon rivers. It is one of the two cities in the Amazon rainforest with a population of over 1 million people, alongside Belém. The city was founded in 1669 as the Fort of São José do Rio Negro.", "a": "Arne Müseler", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manaus_amazonas.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Norbulingka_on_a_sunny_day.jpg/1280px-Norbulingka_on_a_sunny_day.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2017, "lat": 29.65388889, "lng": 91.09166667, "p": "Norbulingka, Chengguan District, China", "h": "About this photo: Norbulingka is a palace and surrounding park built from 1755.It served as the traditional summer residence of the successive Dalai Lamas from the 1780s up until the 14th Dalai Lama's exile in 1959.\n\nNorbulingka: Norbulingka is a palace and surrounding park in Lhasa, Tibet built from 1755. It served as the traditional summer residence of the successive Dalai Lamas from the 1780s up until the 14th Dalai Lama's exile in 1959. Part of the \"Historic Ensemble of the Potala Palace\", Norbulingka is recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and was added as an extension of this Historic Ensemble in 2001. It was built by the 7th Dalai Lama and served both as administrative centre and religious centre. It is a unique representation of Tibetan palace architecture. Norbulingka Palace is situated in the west side of Lhasa, a short distance to the southwest of Potala Palace. Norbulingka covers an area of around 36 hectares and considered to be the largest man-made garden in Tibet. Norbulingka park is considered the premier park of all such horticultural parks in similar ethnic settings in Tibet.\n\nThe wider area, Chengguan: Chengguan may refer to: Chengguan , an administrative practice of city-level local governments in China Chengguan , fully known as Qingliang Chengguan , Chinese monk, representative of the Huayan school of Chinese Buddhism", "a": "travel oriented", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Norbulingka_on_a_sunny_day.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Leicester_Square_-_May_2026.jpg/1280px-Leicester_Square_-_May_2026.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2026, "lat": 51.5104, "lng": -0.1301, "p": "Leicester Square, City of Westminster, United Kingdom", "h": "About this photo: Leicester Square , London\n\nLeicester Square: Leicester Square is a pedestrianised square in the West End of London, England, and is the centre of London's entertainment district. It was laid out in 1670 as Leicester Fields, which was named after the recently built Leicester House, itself named after Robert Sidney, 2nd Earl of Leicester. The square was originally a gentrified residential area, with tenants including Frederick, Prince of Wales and the artists William Hogarth and Joshua Reynolds. It became more down-market in the late 18th century as Leicester House was demolished and retail developments took place, becoming a centre for entertainment. Major theatres were built in the 19th century, which were converted to cinemas towards the middle of the next.\n\nThe wider area, City of Westminster: The City of Westminster is a London borough with city status in Greater London, England. It is the site of the United Kingdom's Houses of Parliament and much of the British government. It contains a large part of central London, including most of the West End, such as the major shopping areas around Oxford Street, Regent Street, Piccadilly and Bond Street, and the entertainment district of Soho.", "a": "APK", "l": "CC BY 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leicester_Square_-_May_2026.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Smith_Street_Mall%2C_Darwin%2C_2023_%2802%29.jpg/1280px-Smith_Street_Mall%2C_Darwin%2C_2023_%2802%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2023, "lat": -12.46150833, "lng": 130.83992778, "p": "Smith Street, Darwin, Darwin, Australia", "h": "About this photo: View of Smith Street Mall, Darwin, Northern Territory\n\nSmith Street, Darwin: Smith Street is a major street in the Darwin Central Business district of Darwin, Northern Territory, Australia. Smith Street is named after A. H. Smith who was the first surveyor in charge of party number 3 of George Goyder in 1869. Smith Street runs 2.2 km in a north-west to south-east direction from Larrakeyah into the Darwin CBD. The street intersects with Daly Street, Knuckey Street and terminates at the Esplanade.\n\nThe wider area, Darwin: Darwin may refer to: Charles Darwin , English naturalist and writer, originator of the theory of biological evolution by natural selection Darwin, Northern Territory, a capital city in Australia, named after the naturalist Darwin may also refer to:", "a": "Bahnfrend", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smith_Street_Mall,_Darwin,_2023_(02).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Keihan_Naniwabashi_Station.jpg/1280px-Keihan_Naniwabashi_Station.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2020, "lat": 34.692972, "lng": 135.505937, "p": "Naniwabashi Station, Osaka, Japan", "h": "Naniwabashi Station: Naniwabashi Station is a railway station on the Keihan Nakanoshima Line in Kita-ku, Osaka, Japan. It opened on October 19, 2008 .\n\nThe wider area, Osaka: Osaka , ó̞ó̞-] ) is a designated city in the Kansai region of Honshu in Japan. It is the capital of and most populous city in Osaka Prefecture, and the third-most populous city proper in Japan, following the special wards of Tokyo and Yokohama.", "a": "KishujiRapid", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keihan_Naniwabashi_Station.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Plaza_Murillo_en_La_Paz_-_Bolivia.jpg/1280px-Plaza_Murillo_en_La_Paz_-_Bolivia.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2025, "lat": -16.4958, "lng": -68.1336, "p": "Plaza Murillo, La Paz, Bolivia", "h": "About this photo: Plaza Pedro Domingo \"Murillo\" ubicada en la ciudad de Nuestra Señora de La Paz - Bolivia\n\nPlaza Murillo: The Plaza Murillo is the central plaza of the city of La Paz and the open space most connected to the political life of Bolivia. Prominent buildings on the plaza include the Palacio Quemado, the Casa Grande del Pueblo, Plurinational Legislative Assembly of Bolivia, and the Cathedral of La Paz . It is located in the old town, or Casco Viejo, of the city and is surrounded by Socabaya Street to the west, Ayacucho Street to the east, Comercio Street to the south, and a continuation of Ingavi and Ballivan Streets to the north.\n\nThe wider area, La Paz: La Paz , officially Nuestra Señora de La Paz , is the seat of government of Bolivia. With 755,732 residents as of 2024, it is the third-most populous city in Bolivia. Its metropolitan area, which includes the neighboring city of El Alto, and other smaller towns, is the second most populous urban area in Bolivia, with a population of 2.2 million, after Santa Cruz de la Sierra with a population of 2.3 million.", "a": "Parallelepiped09", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaza_Murillo_en_La_Paz_-_Bolivia.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Chamber_of_commerce_Hamburg_Germany.JPG/1280px-Chamber_of_commerce_Hamburg_Germany.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2006, "lat": 53.54972222, "lng": 9.99083333, "p": "Hamburg Chamber of Commerce, Hamburg, Germany", "h": "About this photo: Chamber of commerce , Hamburg, Germany.\n\nHamburg Chamber of Commerce: The Hamburg Chamber of Commerce , originally named the Commercial Deputation , is the chamber of commerce for the city state of Hamburg, and was founded in 1665. Hamburg has for centuries been a commercial centre of Northern Europe, and the Hamburg Chamber of Commerce currently has 160,000 companies as its members. It was traditionally one of the three main political bodies of Hamburg.\n\nThe wider area, Hamburg: Hamburg, officially the Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg, is the second-largest city in Germany after Berlin and sixth-largest city in the European Union, with a population of over 1.9 million. The Hamburg Metropolitan Region has a population of over 5.1 million and is the tenth-largest metropolitan region by GDP in the European Union.", "a": "User: (WT-shared) Riggwelter at wts wikivoyage", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chamber_of_commerce_Hamburg_Germany.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Gran_Teatro_de_La_Habana_Alicia_Alonso.jpg/1280px-Gran_Teatro_de_La_Habana_Alicia_Alonso.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 23.1369, "lng": -82.3594, "p": "Gran Teatro de La Habana, Havana, Cuba", "h": "About this photo: Gran Teatro de La Habana Alicia Alonso\n\nGran Teatro de La Habana: Gran Teatro de La Habana is a theater in Havana, Cuba, home to the Cuban National Ballet. It was designed by the Belgian architect Paul Belau and built by Purdy and Henderson, Engineers in 1914 at the site of the former Teatro Tacón. Its construction was paid for by the Galician immigrants of Havana to serve as a community-social center. Located in the Paseo del Prado, its facilities include theatres, a concert hall, conference rooms, a video screening room, as well as an art gallery, a choral center and several rehearsal halls for dance companies. It hosts the International Ballet Festival of Havana every two years since 1960. In the 1910s, the other historic Tacón Theatre was partially demolished and the performance hall was incorporated into the current Great Theatre of Havana.\n\nThe wider area, Havana: Havana is the capital and largest city of Cuba. The heart of La Habana Province, Havana is the country's main port and commercial center. It is the most populous city, the largest by area, and the second-largest metropolitan area in the Caribbean region. The population in 2021 was 2,142,939 inhabitants, and its area is 728.26 km2 for the capital city and 8,475.57 km2 for the metropolitan zone.", "a": "Tacorontey", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gran_Teatro_de_La_Habana_Alicia_Alonso.jpg"}, {"u": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Jerusalemstation1978.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled", "y": 1978, "lat": 31.76685, "lng": 35.22471111, "p": "Jerusalem–Khan railway station, Jerusalem, Israel", "h": "About this photo: Jerusalem Railway Station, 1978\n\nJerusalem–Khan railway station: The Jerusalem railway station is a historic railway station in Jerusalem, located between Hebron Road and Bethlehem Road, near the German Colony. It is also known as the Jerusalem–Khan railway station after the caravanserai building, now the Khan Theater located across the road, to differentiate it from the Jerusalem–Malha and Jerusalem–Yitzhak Navon stations opened after its closure. It served as the eastern terminus of the Jaffa–Jerusalem railway until its closure in 1998. The station opened in 1892 during the Ottoman period at an elevation of 787 metres on the 86.6 kilometer mark of the Jaffa–Jerusalem line. In 1998 this railway along with the station were closed and the station was not included in the restoration of the Tel Aviv – Jerusalem line, completed in 2005, at which point Jerusalem–Malha station became the line's eastern terminus.\n\nThe wider area, Jerusalem: Jerusalem is a city in the Southern Levant, on a plateau in the Judaean Mountains between the Mediterranean and the Dead Sea. It is one of the oldest cities in the world and is considered a holy city to the three major Abrahamic religions: Judaism, Christianity and Islam.", "a": "Bettsy1970", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalemstation1978.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Downtown_Vancouver_Sunset.jpg/1280px-Downtown_Vancouver_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": 49.281762, "lng": -123.117718, "p": "Financial District, Vancouver, Vancouver, Canada", "h": "About this photo: View of downtown Vancouver from the Lookout Tower at Harbour Centre\n\nFinancial District, Vancouver: The Financial District is the central business district of Downtown Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada. It is located on the Downtown Peninsula, bounded roughly by Burrard Street and West Hastings Street. It is one of the densest areas of Vancouver, containing more than 60% of the city's office space, and is home to many major banks, corporate headquarters, accounting and law firms, and financial services companies. The area also contains various government buildings, embassies and consulates, non-governmental organizations, and luxury hotels.\n\nThe wider area, Vancouver: Vancouver is a major city in Western Canada, located in the Lower Mainland region of British Columbia. As the most populous city in the province, the 2021 Canadian census recorded 662,248 people in the city, up from 631,486 in 2016. The Greater Vancouver area had a population of 2.6 million in 2021, making it the third-largest metropolitan area in Canada after Toronto and Montreal.", "a": "MagnusL3D", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Vancouver_Sunset.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Christmas_market_in_Tallinn.jpg/1280px-Christmas_market_in_Tallinn.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2015, "lat": 59.4373, "lng": 24.7457, "p": "Tallinn Christmas Market, Tallinn, Estonia", "h": "About this photo: Christmas market in Town Hall Square of Tallinn Old Town . Right: Tallinn Town Hall .\n\nTallinn Christmas Market: Tallinn Christmas Market is a Christmas market held every year in Tallinn, Estonia. The modern market was founded in 2001 by Paul Oberschneider, and is open from Christmas Day until Epiphany. The latter also marks the end of the Christmas season according to the Gregorian calendar. The market brings tourists from all over the world to Tallinn. This is traditionally held on the old Town Hall Square. Christmas cabins and stands sell handcrafts, hot wine and souvenirs to about 200,000 visitors every year. In 2024 Estonian Public Broadcasting described the market as \"tacky\" with kitschy elements resembling a souvenir shop.\n\nThe wider area, Tallinn: Tallinn is the capital and largest city of Estonia. Located on a bay in northern Estonia, on the shore of the Gulf of Finland of the Baltic Sea, it has a population of 452,563 as of 2026, making it the third most-populous city in the Baltics. As of 2024, the population of the Tallinn metropolitan area is estimated at 646,315. Tallinn is the main governmental, financial, industrial, and cultural centre of Estonia.", "a": "Maasaak", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christmas_market_in_Tallinn.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/2010_07_22230_6939_Xinyi_District%2C_Taipei%2C_Buildings%2C_Taipei_101%2C_Observation%2C_Streets_in_Taipei%2C_Taiwan.JPG/1280px-2010_07_22230_6939_Xinyi_District%2C_Taipei%2C_Buildings%2C_Taipei_101%2C_Observation%2C_Streets_in_Taipei%2C_Taiwan.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": 25.0269, "lng": 121.5766, "p": "Xiangshan, Taipei, Taipei, Taiwan", "h": "Xiangshan, Taipei: Xiangshan , known in English as Elephant Mountain or Mount Elephant, is a mountain in Xinyi District, Taipei, Taiwan. It is close to the Taipei Metro Xiangshan Station. It is 183 m high and has a hiking trail to the peak, covering a distance of about 1.5 km . Taipei 101 can be seen from the trail. The Six Giant Rocks on the peak are a tourist attraction in Xiangshan, and there are platforms for photographers.\n\nThe wider area, Taipei: Taipei , officially Taipei City, is the capital and a special municipality of Taiwan. Located in Northern Taiwan, Taipei City is an enclave of the municipality of New Taipei City that sits about 25 kilometres southwest of the northern port city of Keelung. Most of the city rests on the Taipei Basin, an ancient lakebed.", "a": "Lord Koxinga", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010_07_22230_6939_Xinyi_District,_Taipei,_Buildings,_Taipei_101,_Observation,_Streets_in_Taipei,_Taiwan.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Palais_de_la_bourse_%C3%A0_Marseille.JPG/1280px-Palais_de_la_bourse_%C3%A0_Marseille.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2008, "lat": 43.29583333, "lng": 5.37527778, "p": "Palais de la Bourse (Marseille), Marseille, France", "h": "About this photo: Palais de la bourse à Marseille, façade sur la canebière\n\nPalais de la Bourse (Marseille): The Palais de la Bourse is a building on the Canebière in Marseille, France. It houses the Chamber of Commerce and Industry Aix Marseille Provence, as well as the Musée de la Marine et de l'Économie de Marseille.\n\nThe wider area, Marseille: Marseille is a city in southern France, the prefecture of the Bouches-du-Rhône department and of the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region. Situated in Provence, it marks the eastern end of the Côte Bleue on the Mediterranean coast. Marseille is the second-most populous city proper in France, after Paris, with 886,040 people in 2023 over a municipal territory of 241 km2 .", "a": "Robert Valette", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palais_de_la_bourse_%C3%A0_Marseille.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Saint_John_the_Baptist_Church%2C_Yerevan_03.jpg/1280px-Saint_John_the_Baptist_Church%2C_Yerevan_03.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2017, "lat": 40.185265, "lng": 44.503673, "p": "Saint John the Baptist Church, Yerevan, Yerevan, Armenia", "h": "About this photo: This is a photo of a monument in Armenia identified by the ID\n\nSaint John the Baptist Church, Yerevan: Saint John the Baptist Church is an active church in the old area of Kond, Yerevan, Armenia. First, it was built on the height of Kond district, in 1710, in the place of a medieval church ruined as the result of a 1689 earthquake. It was built by a rich man, Melik Aghamal, living in Yerevan. Like the other medieval churches, this is a three-nave basilic church. The rectangular plan of the church includes the prayer-hall and the main altar on the eastern side, attached to which are the sacristies.\n\nThe wider area, Yerevan: Yerevan is the capital and largest city of Armenia, as well as one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities. Situated along the Hrazdan River, Yerevan is the administrative, cultural, and industrial centre of the country, as its primate city. It has been the capital since 1918, the fourteenth in the history of Armenia and the seventh located in or around the Ararat Plain.", "a": "AKB7", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_John_the_Baptist_Church,_Yerevan_03.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Rue_du_B%C5%93uf_%28Lyon%2C_2025%29.jpg/1280px-Rue_du_B%C5%93uf_%28Lyon%2C_2025%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2025, "lat": 45.7625, "lng": 4.82694444, "p": "Rue du Bœuf, Lyon, France", "h": "Rue du Bœuf: The Rue du Bœuf is a 188-metre cobbled pedestrian street of the Vieux Lyon quarter, located in the 5th arrondissement of Lyon. Very representative of the Renaissance architecture of the neighborhood, it is lined only with old houses from the 16th or 17th century. The street connects the Rue de Gadagne which it continues after the Place du Petit Collège and the intersection of the Rue du Chemin Neuf, the Rue de la Bombarde and the Rue Tramassac which prolongs it. The street belongs to the zone classified as World Heritage Site by UNESCO.\n\nThe wider area, Lyon: Lyon is a city in France. It is located at the confluence of the rivers Rhône and Saône, to the northwest of the French Alps, 391 km southeast of Paris, 278 km north of Marseille, and 113 km southwest of Geneva, Switzerland.", "a": "Corentin Eustacchi", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rue_du_B%C5%93uf_(Lyon,_2025).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Lisbon_One_-_82_%283466307459%29.jpg/1280px-Lisbon_One_-_82_%283466307459%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": 38.71277778, "lng": -9.13888889, "p": "Praça da Figueira, Lisbon, Portugal", "h": "Praça da Figueira: The Praça da Figueira is a large square in the centre of Lisbon, in Portugal. It is part of the Lisbon Baixa, the area of the city reurbanised after the 1755 Lisbon earthquake. In the 16th century the square did not exist, and most of its area was occupied by the Hospital Real de Todos os Santos , the most important in the city. In 1755, after the great earthquake which destroyed most of Lisbon, the hospital was greatly damaged. It was demolished around 1775. The large area previously occupied by the hospital in the Baixa was turned into an open market square. Around 1885, a large covered market of 8,000 m2 was built. This market existed until 1949, when it was demolished. Since then the square has been an open space. In 1971 a bronze equestrian statue representing King John I , by sculptor Leopoldo de Almeida, was inaugurated in the square.\n\nThe wider area, Lisbon: Lisbon is the capital and most populous city of Portugal, with an estimated population of 658,236 as of 2025, within its administrative limits and 3,353,000 within the metropolis, as of 2025. The city lies in the western part of the Iberian Peninsula, fronting the northern shore of the River Tagus.", "a": "Kyle Taylor from London, 84 Countries", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lisbon_One_-_82_(3466307459).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Donkeys%2C_Timbuktu.jpg/1280px-Donkeys%2C_Timbuktu.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2007, "lat": 16.77583333, "lng": -3.00944444, "p": "Timbuktu, Mali", "h": "About this photo: Timbuktu, preferred method of transport, Mali, W. Africa, The team of donkeys walks past the mud mosque laden with their heavy items of transport. The mud wall's protruding wood beams and the relaxed gait of the animals and people add to the gentle scene.\n\nTimbuktu: Timbuktu is a city in Mali, situated 20 kilometres north of the Niger River. It is the capital of the Tombouctou Region, one of the 19 administrative regions of Mali, with a population of around eighty-four thousand people in the 2023 census. Archaeological evidence suggests prehistoric settlements in the region, predating the city's Islamic scholarly and trade prominence in the medieval period. Timbuktu began as a seasonal settlement and became permanent early in the 12th century. After a shift in trading routes, particularly after the visit by Mansa Musa around 1325, Timbuktu flourished, due to its strategic location, from the trade in salt, gold, and ivory. It gradually expanded as an important Islamic city on the Saharan trade route and attracted many scholars and traders before it became part of the Mali Empire early in the 14th century.", "a": "Flickr user: Emilio Labrador Santiago de Chile https://www.flickr.com/people/305", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donkeys,_Timbuktu.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/BASA-1577K-1-61-14-Sofia%2C_Bulgaria_%28cropped%29.JPG/1280px-BASA-1577K-1-61-14-Sofia%2C_Bulgaria_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1926, "lat": 42.6967, "lng": 23.32137, "p": "St. Nedelya Church bombing, Sofia, Bulgaria", "h": "About this photo: St Nedelya Church after the assault on 16 April 1925 , Sofia, Bulgaria.\n\nSt. Nedelya Church bombing: The St Nedelya Church bombing was a terrorist attack on St Nedelya Church in Sofia, Bulgaria. It was carried out on 16 April 1925, when a group of the Military Organisation of the Bulgarian Communist Party directed and supplied by the Soviet Military Intelligence blew up the church's roof during the funeral service of General Konstantin Georgiev, who had been killed in a previous communist assault on 14 April. Over 200 people, mainly from the country's political and military elite, were killed in the attack and around 500 bystander worshipers, who attended the liturgy, were injured. The bombing is by far the deadliest terrorist act in Bulgarian history and the deadliest in all of Europe until the Air India Flight 182 in 1985 60 years later.\n\nThe wider area, Sofia: Sofia is the capital and largest city of Bulgaria. It is situated in the Sofia Valley at the foot of the Vitosha mountain, in the western part of the country. The city is built west of the Iskar river and has many mineral springs, such as the Sofia Central Mineral Baths. It has a humid continental climate. Known as Serdica in antiquity, Sofia has been an area of human habitation since at least 7000 BC.", "a": "Unknown author Unknown author", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BASA-1577K-1-61-14-Sofia,_Bulgaria_(cropped).JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Hagia_Irene%2C_Istanbul_%2852112279404%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Hagia_Irene%2C_Istanbul_%2852112279404%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2022, "lat": 41.00972222, "lng": 28.98111111, "p": "Hagia Irene, Istanbul, Turkey", "h": "About this photo: Eastern Orthodox church located in the outer courtyard of Topkapı Palace. The building stands on the site of a pre-Christian temple. It ranks as the first church building completed in Constantinople, before Hagia Sophia. The first was burned down and the new one was built in the 6th century.\n\nHagia Irene: Hagia Irene or Hagia Eirene , sometimes known also as Saint Irene, is a former Eastern Orthodox church located in the outer courtyard of Topkapı Palace in Istanbul. It is the oldest known church structure in the city and one of only two Byzantine churches still standing in Istanbul today that were never converted into mosques, alongside the Church of Saint Mary of the Mongols, as it was used as an arsenal for storing weapons until the 19th century. The Hagia Irene now operates as a museum and concert hall.\n\nThe wider area, Istanbul: Istanbul, historically known as Constantinople, is the largest city in Turkey, a megacity, constituting the country's economic, cultural, and historical center. With a population of over 15 million, it is home to 18% of the population of Turkey. Istanbul is among the largest cities in Europe and in the world by population.", "a": "Ninara", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Irene,_Istanbul_(52112279404)_(cropped).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Budapest%2C_Magyar_Tudom%C3%A1nyos_Akad%C3%A9mia_24.jpeg/1280px-Budapest%2C_Magyar_Tudom%C3%A1nyos_Akad%C3%A9mia_24.jpeg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 47.5011, "lng": 19.0464, "p": "Hungarian Academy of Sciences, Budapest, Hungary", "h": "About this photo: Budapest, Sitz der Ungarischen Akademie der Wissenschaften\n\nHungarian Academy of Sciences: The Hungarian Academy of Sciences is the most important and prestigious learned society of Hungary. Its seat is at the bank of the Danube in Budapest, between Széchenyi rakpart and Akadémia utca. Its main responsibilities are the cultivation of science, dissemination of scientific findings, supporting research and development, and representing Hungarian science domestically and around the world.\n\nThe wider area, Budapest: Budapest is the capital and largest city of Hungary. It is Hungary's primate city with 1.7 million inhabitants and its greater metro area has a population of about 3.3 million, representing one-third of the country's population and producing more than 40% of the country's economic output.", "a": "Aisano", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Budapest,_Magyar_Tudom%C3%A1nyos_Akad%C3%A9mia_24.jpeg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Detroit_Photographic_Company_%280985%29.jpg/1280px-Detroit_Photographic_Company_%280985%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1900, "lat": 23.13327222, "lng": -82.35836111, "p": "Fuente de la India, Havana, Cuba", "h": "About this photo: 1 photographic print , ca. 17 x 23 cm. No. 53498 Published by the Detroit Photographic Company .\n\nFuente de la India: Fuente de la India is a fountain by Giuseppe Gaggini in Havana, Cuba, at the south extreme of Paseo del Prado, about 100 m south of El Capitolio, between Monte and Dragones Streets. The figure represents the Indian woman \"Habana\" in whose honor Havana was named. Originally it was placed outside the city walls at the end of the Alameda which today is the Paseo del Prado. From 1803 until that time a statue of King Carlos III was located there. Its opening was an event in the then still young town of San Cristobal. In 1863, by the resolution of Council, the fountain was moved to the Parque Central. In 1875 it was put back in its current position, i.e. its initial one but facing towards the then called Campo de Marte. In 1928, when the adjacent area became the Plaza de la Fraternidad, the statue was rotated 90° to face the new Capitolio building.\n\nThe wider area, Havana: Havana is the capital and largest city of Cuba. The heart of La Habana Province, Havana is the country's main port and commercial center. It is the most populous city, the largest by area, and the second-largest metropolitan area in the Caribbean region. The population in 2021 was 2,142,939 inhabitants, and its area is 728.26 km2 for the capital city and 8,475.57 km2 for the metropolitan zone.", "a": "Detroit Publishing Company", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detroit_Photographic_Company_(0985).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Reconstructed_Rova_Antananarivo_Madagascar.jpg/1280px-Reconstructed_Rova_Antananarivo_Madagascar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": -18.92365, "lng": 47.53202, "p": "Rova of Antananarivo, Antananarivo, Madagascar", "h": "About this photo: View of the reconstructed Manjakamiadana palace, Rova of Antananarivo, Madagascar\n\nRova of Antananarivo: The Rova of Antananarivo is a royal palace complex in Madagascar that was the home of the sovereigns of the Kingdom of Imerina in the 17th and 18th centuries, as well as of the rulers of the Kingdom of Madagascar in the 19th century. Located in the central highland city of Antananarivo, the Rova occupies the highest point on Analamanga, formerly the highest of Antananarivo's many hills. Merina king Andrianjaka, who ruled Imerina from around 1610 until 1630, is believed to have captured Analamanga from a Vazimba king around 1610 or 1625 and erected the site's first fortified royal structure. Successive Merina kings continued to rule from the site until the fall of the monarchy in 1896, frequently restoring, modifying or adding royal structures within the compound to suit their needs.\n\nThe wider area, Antananarivo: Antananarivo , also known by its colonial shorthand form Tana , is the capital and largest city of Madagascar. The administrative area of the city, known as Antananarivo-Renivohitra , is the capital of Analamanga region. The city sits at 1,280 m above sea level in the center of the island, making it the highest national capital by elevation among the island countries.", "a": "Hery Zo Rakotondramanana", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstructed_Rova_Antananarivo_Madagascar.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/View_from_Al_Qurum_Heights_Rd_%288725226365%29.jpg/1280px-View_from_Al_Qurum_Heights_Rd_%288725226365%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 23.61666667, "lng": 58.49472222, "p": "Qurum, Bawshar Province, Oman", "h": "About this photo: View from Al Qurum Heights Rd\n\nQurum: Qurum is an upscale suburb of Muscat in Oman. Its main attractions are the Qurum Natural Park, the Qurum City Centre and the Qurum Beach.", "a": "Francisco Anzola", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Al_Qurum_Heights_Rd_(8725226365).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Quito_Observatory.JPG/1280px-Quito_Observatory.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2005, "lat": -0.21494, "lng": -78.50258, "p": "Quito Astronomical Observatory, Quito, Ecuador", "h": "About this photo: Quito Observatory, located in the park La Alameda . Established in 1873, it was the first national observatory of South America.\n\nQuito Astronomical Observatory: The Quito Astronomical Observatory is a research institute of EPN, the National Polytechnic School in Quito, Ecuador. Its major research fields are astronomy and atmospheric physics. The Quito Astronomical Observatory is one of the oldest observatories in South America and was founded in 1873. In 1963, The Government of Ecuador transferred title of the Observatory to the National Polytechnic School. The Quito Astronomical Observatory is the National Observatory of Ecuador and is located 12 minutes south of the Equator in the Historic Center of Quito.\n\nThe wider area, Quito: Quito , officially San Francisco de Quito, is the capital of Ecuador, and the province of Pichincha. It is the largest city in Pichincha and the second-largest city in Ecuador, with an estimated population of 1.7 million in the city and 2.8 million in its metropolitan area. Quito is in a valley on the eastern slopes of Pichincha, an active stratovolcano in the Andes.", "a": "Marc Figueras Oersted (let's talk)", "l": "CC BY 2.5", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quito_Observatory.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Arena_Fonte_Nova_view_from_lake_%28zoom%29.jpg/1280px-Arena_Fonte_Nova_view_from_lake_%28zoom%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": -12.97861111, "lng": -38.50416667, "p": "Arena Fonte Nova, Salvador, Brazil", "h": "About this photo: Arena Fonte Nova, Stadion für die WM 2014\n\nArena Fonte Nova: The Arena Acelen Fonte Nova is a football-specific stadium located in Salvador, Bahia, Brazil with a maximum capacity of 47,902 people. The stadium was built in place of the older Estádio Fonte Nova. Its primary tenant is Esporte Clube Bahia. The stadium was first used for the 2013 FIFA Confederations Cup and the subsequent 2014 FIFA World Cup, including the 5–1 win of The Netherlands over reigning World Champions Spain. The stadium was used as one of the venues for the football competition of the 2016 Summer Olympics held in Rio de Janeiro. The stadium was also selected to host matches for the 2019 Copa America. It is included in the list of venues bidding to host matches for the 2027 FIFA Women's World Cup.\n\nThe wider area, Salvador: Salvador, meaning \"salvation\" in Catalan, Spanish, and Portuguese may refer to: Salvador", "a": "Faquini Produção Fotográfica; Fotógrafo David Campbell;", "l": "CC BY 3.0 br", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arena_Fonte_Nova_view_from_lake_(zoom).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Building_of_Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Mo%C3%A7ambique.jpg/1280px-Building_of_Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Mo%C3%A7ambique.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2015, "lat": -25.9736, "lng": 32.5731, "p": "National Library of Mozambique, Maputo, Mozambique", "h": "About this photo: Building of Biblioteca Nacional de Moçambique in Maputo\n\nNational Library of Mozambique: The National Library of Mozambique is located in Maputo, Mozambique. It is located in a structure which formerly housed the Office of Finance of the Colony of Mozambique. It was designed by the architect Mario Veiga in 1904, and is located on 25 de Setembro Avenue.\n\nThe wider area, Maputo: Maputo is the capital and largest city of Mozambique. Located near the southern end of the country, it is within 120 kilometres of the borders with Eswatini and South Africa. The city has a population of 1,088,449 distributed over a land area of 347.69 km2 . The Maputo metropolitan area includes the neighbouring city of Matola, and has a total population of 2,717,437.", "a": "Claus Wonnemann", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Building_of_Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Mo%C3%A7ambique.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/2017_Santiago_de_Chile_-_calle_Concha_y_Toro%2C_Santiago_Centro.jpg/1280px-2017_Santiago_de_Chile_-_calle_Concha_y_Toro%2C_Santiago_Centro.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2017, "lat": -33.44557778, "lng": -70.66591667, "p": "Barrio Concha y Toro, Santiago, Chile", "h": "About this photo: 2017 in Santiago de Chile\n\nBarrio Concha y Toro: Barrio Concha y Toro is a barrio in Santiago, Chile, with a medieval-like street plan influenced by the ideas of Camillo Sitte. The houses are built on the former site of the Díaz Gana Palace's park, which was divided into lots in 1915. The barrio's boundaries are defined by Brasil Avenue, Avenida Libertador General Bernardo O'Higgins, Cumming Avenue, Romero Street, Maturana Street and Erasmo Escala Street.\n\nThe wider area, Santiago: Santiago , also known as Santiago de Chile , is the capital and largest city of Chile and one of the largest cities in the Americas. Located in the Chilean Central Valley within the Santiago Basin, between the Andes to the east and the Chilean Coastal Range to the west, it anchors the Santiago Metropolitan Region and its conurbation of Greater Santiago, which comprises more than forty communes and concentrates over…", "a": "Felipe Restrepo Acosta", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2017_Santiago_de_Chile_-_calle_Concha_y_Toro,_Santiago_Centro.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/1962_Cornhill3_Boston_byCervinRobinson_HABS_MA787.jpg/1280px-1962_Cornhill3_Boston_byCervinRobinson_HABS_MA787.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1962, "lat": 42.35960556, "lng": -71.057725, "p": "Cornhill (Government Center, Boston), Boston, United States", "h": "About this photo: Cornhill, Boston, Mass., 1962. From the Historic American Buildings Survey, Library of Congress, Prints and Photograph Division, Washington, D.C. 20540 USA. Survey number HABS MA-787. http://hdl.loc.gov/loc.pnp/hhh.ma0480\n\nCornhill (Government Center, Boston): Cornhill was a street that existed in Boston, Massachusetts, United States, between the 18th and 20th centuries. It was located on the site of the current City Hall Plaza in Government Center. It was named in 1829; previously it was known as Market Street . In its time, it comprised a busy part of the city near Brattle Street, Court Street and Scollay Square. In the 19th century, it was the home of many bookstores and publishing companies. As of 1969, Cornhill exists as 144 feet along the edge of City Hall Plaza.\n\nThe wider area, Boston: Boston is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Massachusetts. It serves as a cultural and financial center of New England, a region of the Northeastern United States. Boston has an area of 48.4 square miles and a population of 675,647 as of the 2020 census, making it the third-most populous city in the Northeastern United States after New York City and Philadelphia.", "a": "Cervin Robinson, photographer.", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1962_Cornhill3_Boston_byCervinRobinson_HABS_MA787.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/National_Amusement_Park_in_Ulaanbaatar.jpg/1280px-National_Amusement_Park_in_Ulaanbaatar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2025, "lat": 47.90986111, "lng": 106.92191667, "p": "National Amusement Park, Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia", "h": "About this photo: National Amusement Park in Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia\n\nNational Amusement Park: The National Amusement Park is an amusement park in Sükhbaatar District, Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia.\n\nThe wider area, Ulaanbaatar: Ulaanbaatar is the capital and largest city of Mongolia. It has a population of 1.79 million, and is the coldest capital city in the world by average yearly temperature. The municipality is located in north central Mongolia at an elevation of about 1,300 metres in a valley on the Tuul River.", "a": "Quintin Soloviev", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Amusement_Park_in_Ulaanbaatar.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Casa_consistorial_de_Sevilla_2019.jpg/1280px-Casa_consistorial_de_Sevilla_2019.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": 37.3887, "lng": -5.9945, "p": "Seville City Hall, Seville, Spain", "h": "About this photo: Seville City Hall , currently home of the city's government.\n\nSeville City Hall: The Seville City Hall is a Plateresque-style building in Plaza Nueva in Seville , currently housing the City Council of Seville. The building has a large façade divided into five modules, decorated by Plateresque reliefs; these include grotesque motifs inspired by Italian Florentine architecture, heraldry symbols, allegories of Justice and Good Government and depictions of mythological or historical characters such as Hercules, Julius Caesar and Charles V.\n\nThe wider area, Seville: Seville is the capital and largest city of the autonomous community of Andalusia and the province of Seville in Spain. It is situated on the lower reaches of the River Guadalquivir, in the southwest of the Iberian Peninsula. Seville has a municipal population of about 688,714 as of 2025, and a metropolitan population of about 1.5 million, making it the largest city in Andalusia and the 5th-largest city in Spain.", "a": "Gzzz", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_consistorial_de_Sevilla_2019.jpg"}, {"u": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/57/Hotel_Vancouver_built_1887_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled", "y": 1888, "lat": 49.282631, "lng": -123.118865, "p": "Hotel Vancouver (1888), Vancouver, Canada", "h": "About this photo: Hotel Vancouver, built 1887 and designed for Canadian Pacific Railway by Thomas Charles Sorby\n\nHotel Vancouver (1888): Hotel Vancouver was a hotel in Vancouver, British Columbia that operated between 1888 and 1913. The hotel was built by the Canadian Pacific Railway and was designed by Thomas Charles Sorby. Originally the railway had planned a much larger hotel, but would up scaling back its plans and built a reduced design. An addition was built in 1893, however, by the turn of the century, the hotel was deemed inadequate. In 1900, the railway held a design competition for a new hotel, and the winning entry announced in 1901 was by Francis Rattenbury. Later in 1901, the railway passed over Rattenbury's winning design and asked him to provide a more inexpensive plan. In 1902, Rattenbury's new design was built partially but never completed. In 1912, Canadian Pacific commissioned a new hotel by Francis S. Swales.\n\nThe wider area, Vancouver: Vancouver is a major city in Western Canada, located in the Lower Mainland region of British Columbia. As the most populous city in the province, the 2021 Canadian census recorded 662,248 people in the city, up from 631,486 in 2016. The Greater Vancouver area had a population of 2.6 million in 2021, making it the third-largest metropolitan area in Canada after Toronto and Montreal.", "a": "Unknown author Unknown author", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotel_Vancouver_built_1887_(1).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Marlborough_Luas_stop.jpg/1280px-Marlborough_Luas_stop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2024, "lat": 53.349235, "lng": -6.25775622, "p": "Marlborough Luas stop, Dublin, Ireland", "h": "About this photo: Green Line luas stop on Marlborough, Dublin\n\nMarlborough Luas stop: Marlborough is a stop on the Luas light-rail tram system in Dublin, Ireland. It opened in 2017 as a stop on Luas Cross City, an extension of the Green Line through the city centre from St. Stephen's Green to Parnell or Broombridge. It is located on Marlborough Street north of the junction with Abbey Street and provides access to Saint Mary's Cathedral.\n\nThe wider area, Dublin: Dublin is the capital and largest city of Ireland. Situated on Dublin Bay at the mouth of the River Liffey, it is in the province of Leinster, and is bordered on the south by the Dublin Mountains, part of the Wicklow Mountains range.", "a": "Jacobfrid", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marlborough_Luas_stop.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Ariana_Cinema_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Ariana_Cinema_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": 34.51956622, "lng": 69.18034978, "p": "Ariana Cinema, Kabul, Afghanistan", "h": "Ariana Cinema: Ariana Cinema was a movie theater in Kabul, Afghanistan located near the Pashtunistan Square on the north side of the Kabul river bank. It was considered to be the second oldest cinema in Kabul city center, after Behzad Cinema. It was publicly owned.\n\nThe wider area, Kabul: Kabul is the capital and largest city of Afghanistan. It is within the jurisdiction of Kabul District and has an estimated population of 5,333,284 people. Located in the eastern half of the country, forming part of the Kabul Province, the city is administratively divided into five zones and 22 municipal districts. The current mayor of the city is Mawlawi Abdul Rashid.", "a": "Masoud Akbari", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariana_Cinema_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Trang_Tien_Plaza.jpg/1280px-Trang_Tien_Plaza.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 21.02472222, "lng": 105.85333333, "p": "Trang Tien Plaza, Hà Nội, Vietnam", "h": "About this photo: Trang Tien Plaza building, Hanoi.\n\nTrang Tien Plaza: Tràng Tiền Plaza or also known as Tràng Tiền Department Store is a department store with 3 facades located on streets of Tràng Tiền, Hàng Bài and Hai Bà Trưng in Hoàn Kiếm district, the downtown of Hanoi, Vietnam. The complex's construction began on April 30, 2000 as Vietnam celebrated the 25th anniversary of The liberation of the south of Vietnam. The complex was finished after 18 months of construction.\n\nThe wider area, Hanoi: Hanoi is the capital and second-most populous centrally-governed city of Vietnam. It covers an area of 3,358.6 km2 , and as of 2026 has a population of 8.7 million. Hanoi had the second-highest gross regional domestic product of all Vietnamese provinces and centrally-governed cities at US$48 billion in 2023, behind Ho Chi Minh City.", "a": "blue_quartz", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trang_Tien_Plaza.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Martin_Melck_House%2C_Strand_Street.jpg/1280px-Martin_Melck_House%2C_Strand_Street.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": -33.91916667, "lng": 18.41972222, "p": "Martin Melck House, Cape Town, South Africa", "h": "About this photo: The Martin Melck House, at 96 Strand Street, Cape Town, was built by the Lutheran Church as a house for their pastor. It was finished in 1782, and first occupied by Rev. Andreas Kolver and his family.\n\nMartin Melck House: The Martin Melck House on 96 Strand Street and the Kostershuis on 100 Strand Street, on either side of the Lutheran Church in Cape Town, are both national heritage sites of South Africa. With the building of the Lutheran church, Martin Melck, a German immigrant whose business success and advantageous marriage rendered him the wealthiest man in Cape Colony in the 18th century, lent part of his property on the east side of the church for a parsonage. He donated the property to the congregation in the end, and in 1781, after Melck's death, construction began on the house. Louis Michel Thibault was probably the original architect. Anton Anreith is generally considered to have been responsible for the stained-glass windows, the swan in the gable, and the engravings on the front door. The building is influenced by French Renaissance architecture.\n\nThe wider area, Cape Town: Cape Town is the legislative capital of South Africa. It is the country's oldest city and the seat of the Parliament of South Africa. Cape Town is the capital and largest city of the Western Cape, and the country's second-most populous city, after Johannesburg. The city is part of the City of Cape Town metropolitan municipality.", "a": "Raymond Ellis", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Melck_House,_Strand_Street.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Andenes_Inkilltambo.jpg/1280px-Andenes_Inkilltambo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2023, "lat": -13.50193611, "lng": -71.95236389, "p": "Inkilltambo, Distrito de Cusco, Peru", "h": "About this photo: Andenes destinados para la agricultura pertenecientes a Inkilltambo\n\nInkilltambo: Inkilltambo, Inquilltambo, Inquiltambo or Inkill Tampu is an archaeological site in Peru. It is situated in the Cusco Region, Cusco Province, San Sebastián District.", "a": "Yanela Sharumi Ccalluco Vargas", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andenes_Inkilltambo.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Oudezijds_Achterburgwal.jpg/1280px-Oudezijds_Achterburgwal.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2023, "lat": 52.37333333, "lng": 4.89805556, "p": "De Wallen, Amsterdam, Netherlands", "h": "About this photo: Oudezijds Achterburgwal in the red-light district\n\nDe Wallen: De Wallen ]) is the largest and best known red-light district in Amsterdam. It consists of a network of alleys containing approximately 300 one-room cabins rented by prostitutes who offer their sexual services from behind a window or glass door, typically illuminated with red lights and blacklight. Window prostitution is the most visible and typical kind of red-light district sex work in Amsterdam. De Wallen, together with prostitution areas Singelgebied and Ruysdaelkade, form the Rosse Buurt of Amsterdam. Of these, De Wallen is the oldest and largest area. It is one of the city's major tourist attractions. Thursday through Sunday access to De Wallen is restricted at 1:00 am, with bars and restaurants closing at 2:00 am and brothels at 3:00 am.\n\nThe wider area, Amsterdam: Amsterdam is the capital and largest city of the Netherlands. As of 1 January 2026, it had a population of 941,873 within the city proper, 1,457,018 in the urban area and 2,480,394 in the metropolitan area. Located in the Dutch province of North Holland, Amsterdam is colloquially referred to as the \"Venice of the North\", for its large number of canals, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.", "a": "APK", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oudezijds_Achterburgwal.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Downtown%2C_Casablanca.jpg/1280px-Downtown%2C_Casablanca.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 33.5949, "lng": -7.6188, "p": "United Nations Square (Casablanca), Casablanca, Morocco", "h": "About this photo: Crossing Boulevard Hassan I with the Avenue des FAR, seen from the place of the United Nations, Casablanca\n\nUnited Nations Square (Casablanca): United Nations Square is a public square in the center of Casablanca, Morocco. It has been central in the history of Casablanca.\n\nThe wider area, Casablanca: Casablanca is the largest city in Morocco and the country's economic and business centre. Located on the Atlantic coast of the Chaouia plain in the central-western part of Morocco, the city has a population of about 3.22 million in the urban area, and over 4.27 million in Greater Casablanca, making it the most populous city in the Maghreb region, and the ninth-largest in the Arab world.", "a": "Achalhikarim", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown,_Casablanca.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Rathausmarkt2.jpg/1280px-Rathausmarkt2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1975, "lat": 53.55027778, "lng": 9.99222222, "p": "Trams in Hamburg, Hamburg, Germany", "h": "About this photo: Straßenbahn auf dem Rathausmarkt Hamburg\n\nTrams in Hamburg: The Hamburg tramway network once formed part of the public transport system in the city and federal state of Hamburg, Germany. Opened in 1866, the network lasted until 1978.\n\nThe wider area, Hamburg: Hamburg, officially the Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg, is the second-largest city in Germany after Berlin and sixth-largest city in the European Union, with a population of over 1.9 million. The Hamburg Metropolitan Region has a population of over 5.1 million and is the tenth-largest metropolitan region by GDP in the European Union.", "a": "Norbirt", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rathausmarkt2.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Jacobsgatan_1963.jpg/1280px-Jacobsgatan_1963.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1963, "lat": 59.33038889, "lng": 18.06969444, "p": "Jakobsgatan, Stockholm, Sweden", "h": "About this photo: Jakobsgatan 20 i Stockholm, restaurang \"Löwenbräu\" har sommarstängd\n\nJakobsgatan: Jakobsgatan is a street at Norrmalm in central Stockholm, Sweden. The street stretches from west–east Tegelbacken in the west to Västra Trädgårdsgatan in the east. At Jakobsgatan several entertainment places can be found such as Hamburger Börs and Centralpalatset.\n\nThe wider area, Stockholm: Stockholm ɔlm] ) is the capital and most populous city of Sweden, as well as the largest urban area in the Nordic countries. Approximately 1 million people live in the municipality, with 1.6 million in the urban area, and 2.5 million in the metropolitan area. The city stretches across fourteen islands where Lake Mälaren flows into the Baltic Sea.", "a": "Holger.Ellgaard", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacobsgatan_1963.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Chicago_Federal_Court%2C_1961.jpg/1280px-Chicago_Federal_Court%2C_1961.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1961, "lat": 41.879191, "lng": -87.629085, "p": "Chicago Federal Building, Chicago, United States", "h": "About this photo: U.S. Courthouse, Chicago, Illinois\n\nChicago Federal Building: The Chicago Federal Building in Chicago, Illinois was constructed between 1898 and 1905 for the purpose of housing the Midwest's federal courts, main post office, and other government bureaus. It stood in The Loop neighborhood on a block bounded by Dearborn, Adams and Clark Streets and Jackson Boulevard. The site held an 1880 post office, courthouse and customhouse which was cleared to make way for the new building. The 1905 building was itself demolished in 1965 and replaced with the Kluczynski Federal Building. The push for a new building was spearheaded by postmaster Washington Hesing with backing by civic leaders and Illinois's members of Congress. The explosion of Chicago's population, especially after the World's Columbian Exposition of 1893, strained the earlier facility beyond capacity.\n\nThe wider area, Chicago: Chicago is the most populous city in the U.S. state of Illinois and in the Midwestern United States. Located on the western shore of Lake Michigan, it is the third-most populous city in the United States and the fifth-most populous city in North America, with a population of 2.74 million at the 2020 census.", "a": "FJC.", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chicago_Federal_Court,_1961.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B2%DB%B3%DB%B0%DB%B8%DB%B1%DB%B5_%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B1%DB%B6%DB%B3%DB%B9_isfahan_Municipality_central_library.jpg/1280px-%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B2%DB%B3%DB%B0%DB%B8%DB%B1%DB%B5_%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B1%DB%B6%DB%B3%DB%B9_isfahan_Municipality_central_library.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2023, "lat": 32.654028, "lng": 51.67234, "p": "Isfahan Central Library and Information Center, Isfahan, Iran", "h": "About this photo: isfahan Municipality central library\n\nIsfahan Central Library and Information Center: Isfahan Central Library and Information Center is a library in the Iranian city of Isfahan. It is 12,000 square metres , and houses 130,000 books. It also has a book club, a media center, a publication section and keeps 6000 manuscripts documents.\n\nThe wider area, Isfahan: Isfahan is a city in the Central District of Isfahan County, Isfahan Province, Iran. It is the capital of the province, the county, and the district. It is located 440 kilometres south of Tehran. The city has a population of approximately 2,238,000, making it the third-most populous city in Iran, after Tehran and Mashhad, and the second-largest metropolitan area.", "a": "Baratiiman", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B2%DB%B3%DB%B0%DB%B8%DB%B1%DB%B5_%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B1%DB%B6%DB%B3%DB%B9_isfahan_Municipality_central_library.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/2_de_mayo_2.jpg/1280px-2_de_mayo_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": -12.04638889, "lng": -77.04277778, "p": "Plaza Dos de Mayo, Lima, Peru", "h": "About this photo: This is a photo of a monument in Peru, identified by ID\n\nPlaza Dos de Mayo: The Dos de Mayo Square , known as the Óvalo de la Reina until 1866, is a public square located in Lima, Peru. Located on the southwestern edge of the historic centre's bufferzone, it serves as the junction of several important avenues, including Alfonso Ugarte, La Colmena and Colonial avenues. In the late 20th century, a vehicular tunnel was built under the square to curb traffic congestions.\n\nThe wider area, Lima: Lima is the capital and largest city of Peru, as well as a primate city. It is located in the valleys of the Chillón, Rímac and Lurín Rivers, in the desert zone of the central coastal part of the country, overlooking the Pacific Ocean. The city is considered the political, cultural, financial and commercial center of Peru.", "a": "Gatodemichi", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2_de_mayo_2.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Panepistimiou_Street_2009.JPG/1280px-Panepistimiou_Street_2009.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": 37.98026667, "lng": 23.73288333, "p": "Panepistimiou Street, Athens, Greece", "h": "About this photo: Panepistimiou Street facing towards Syntagma square . On the left the Propylaea and the main building of the Academy of Athens\n\nPanepistimiou Street: Panepistimiou Street is a major street in Athens that has run one way for non-transit vehicles since 2002 from Vasilissis Amalias Avenue, Syntagma Square and Vassilissis Sofias Avenue to Omonoia Square in which is now a pedestrian crossing and before an intersection. Its total length is about 1.2 km. The street was formally renamed as Eleftherios Venizelos Avenue in 1945 but is still usually known by its historical name. It has six lanes, of which five are for traffic and one eastbound lane for transit buses only. Most of the street runs almost diagonally from southeast to northwest. Buildings along the street include the Bank of Greece, Athens Eye Clinic, the University of Athens, the Academy of Athens, the National Library, the Numismatic Museum, Titania Hotel, Attica Department Store, as well as a part of the Grande Bretagne Hotel and the Catholic Cathedral of Athens.\n\nThe wider area, Athens: Athens is the capital and largest city of Greece. A significant coastal urban area in the Mediterranean, Athens is also the capital of the Attica region and is the southernmost capital on the European mainland. With its urban area's population numbering over 3.6 million, it is the eighth-largest urban area in the European Union .", "a": "Lapost", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panepistimiou_Street_2009.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Teatro_de_la_Comedia_%28Madrid%29_01.jpg/1280px-Teatro_de_la_Comedia_%28Madrid%29_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 40.415569, "lng": -3.700392, "p": "Teatro de la Comedia, Madrid, Spain", "h": "About this photo: Façade of Teatro de la Comedia in Madrid .\n\nTeatro de la Comedia: The Teatro de la Comedia is a theater in Madrid, inaugurated on Calle del Príncipe in 1875 and rebuilt in 1915 after a devastating fire. In 1986 it was designated the headquarters of the National Classical Theater Company and in 1999 it was acquired by said company, along with five other floors of the same building, by the Spanish Ministry of Culture. In October 2015, after being closed for thirteen years for renovation work, it reopened with a performance of The Mayor of Zalamea. After this renovation, the hall has six hundred and thirty seats; in addition, it has a new hall with one hundred seats, called \"Tirso de Molina\".\n\nThe wider area, Madrid: Madrid is the capital and largest city of Spain. It had a population of over 3.4 million in the city proper in 2025, and a metropolitan area population of approximately 6.8 million. Madrid is the second-largest city in the European Union , after Berlin, and its metropolitan area is the second-largest in the EU, after Paris. The municipality covers an area of 605.77 square kilometres .", "a": "Luis García ( Zaqarbal )", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_de_la_Comedia_(Madrid)_01.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Fresco_Chowk_BarnceRoad_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Fresco_Chowk_BarnceRoad_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2014, "lat": 24.8567, "lng": 67.0162, "p": "Burnes Road, Karachi Division, Pakistan", "h": "Burnes Road: Burnes Road, erroneously spelled as Burns Road, and officially known as Muhammad Bin Qasim Road, is a food street located in Karachi, Pakistan. It is well known in the city for its many restaurants and street food vendors.\n\nThe wider area, Karachi Division: Karachi Division is an administrative division shared between the Kohistan and Larr regions of the Sindh Province of Pakistan, created in December 1960. There are seven districts in Karachi Division. The CNIC code of Karachi Division is 42. According to the 2023 Pakistani census, the population of Karachi Division is 20,382,881.", "a": "khadim-un-nabi Rao", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_Chowk_BarnceRoad_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Toronto_Skyline_from_Snake_Island%2C_February_28_2026_%2808%29.jpg/1280px-Toronto_Skyline_from_Snake_Island%2C_February_28_2026_%2808%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2026, "lat": 43.64916667, "lng": -79.37805556, "p": "Old Toronto, Toronto, Canada", "h": "About this photo: View of the Toronto Skyline from Snake Island during winter\n\nOld Toronto: Old Toronto is the part of Toronto, Ontario, Canada, that corresponds to the boundaries of the City of Toronto prior to 1998. It was incorporated as a city in 1834, after being known as the town of York, and being part of York County. Toronto periodically grew in size by annexing adjacent land and municipalities. In 1954, the City of Toronto and the surrounding suburban towns and townships of southern York County were joined in a federation known as the Municipality of Metropolitan Toronto . Later, the lower-tier city annexed two towns until it reached its final boundaries in 1967. In 1998, the municipalities of Metro Toronto were amalgamated into the present-day single-tier City of Toronto, which continues legally from the predecessor City of Toronto, although the by-laws of each municipality remained in force.\n\nThe wider area, Toronto: Toronto is the most populous city in Canada and the capital city of the Canadian province of Ontario. It is located on a harbour at the northwestern shore of Lake Ontario. The city is the fourth-most populous city in North America, behind Mexico City, New York City, and Los Angeles, with a census population of 2,794,356 as of 2021.", "a": "Dillan Payne", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_Skyline_from_Snake_Island,_February_28_2026_(08).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Harrah%27s_Casino%2C_New_Orleans.jpg/1280px-Harrah%27s_Casino%2C_New_Orleans.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": 29.94972222, "lng": -90.06527778, "p": "Caesars New Orleans, New Orleans, United States", "h": "About this photo: Looking down on Harrah's Casino New Orleans from across Canal Street, 2010.\n\nCaesars New Orleans: Caesars New Orleans, formerly Harrah's New Orleans, is a casino in New Orleans, Louisiana, United States, near the foot of Canal Street a block away from the Mississippi River. It is a 115,000 sq ft casino with approximately 2,100 slot machines, over 90 table games and a poker room. The casino is owned by Vici Properties and operated by Caesars Entertainment. Originally opening in 1999 as Harrah's New Orleans, the casino holds the status of being the only land-based casino in the state of Louisiana. It features one of the largest gaming floors in the United States outside of Nevada and several other amenities spread across four city blocks, including eateries from high-profile restaurateurs including Emeril Lagasse and Nobu Matsuhisa, a large sportsbook, a bowling alley, a Manning's sports bar, and two hotel towers.\n\nThe wider area, New Orleans: New Orleans is a consolidated city-parish located along the Mississippi River in the U.S. state of Louisiana. With a population of 383,997 at the 2020 census, New Orleans is the most populous city in Louisiana, the second-most populous in the Deep South , and the twelfth-most populous in the Southeastern United States; the New Orleans metropolitan area, with about 1 million residents, is the 59th-most populous…", "a": "vxla", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harrah%27s_Casino,_New_Orleans.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/ISH_WC_Boston4.jpg/1280px-ISH_WC_Boston4.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2005, "lat": 42.36027778, "lng": -71.05777778, "p": "Boston, United States", "h": "Boston: Boston is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Massachusetts. It serves as a cultural and financial center of New England, a region of the Northeastern United States. Boston has an area of 48.4 square miles and a population of 675,647 as of the 2020 census, making it the third-most populous city in the Northeastern United States after New York City and Philadelphia. The larger Greater Boston metropolitan statistical area had a population of 5 million in 2025, making it the largest metropolis in New England and the eleventh-largest in the United States. Boston was founded on Shawmut Peninsula in 1630 by English Puritan settlers, who named the city after the market town of Boston, Lincolnshire, England.", "a": "Ian Howard", "l": "CC BY 2.5", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISH_WC_Boston4.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Marie-Reine-du-Monde%2C_Montr%C3%A9al.jpg/1280px-Marie-Reine-du-Monde%2C_Montr%C3%A9al.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2017, "lat": 45.4992, "lng": -73.5684, "p": "Archdiocese of Montreal, Montreal, Canada", "h": "About this photo: Marie-Reine-du-Monde, Montréal\n\nArchdiocese of Montreal: The Archdiocese of Montreal is a Latin Church ecclesiastical territory or archdiocese of the Catholic Church in Canada. A metropolitan see, its arch episcopal see is the Montreal, Quebec. It includes Montreal and surrounding areas within Quebec.\n\nThe wider area, Montreal: Montreal is the largest city in the province of Quebec, the second-largest in Canada, and the eighth-largest in North America. Founded in 1642 as Ville-Marie, or \"City of Mary\", it now takes its name from Mount Royal, the triple-peaked mountain around which the early settlement was built. The city is centred on the Island of Montreal and several much smaller, peripheral islands, the largest of which is Île Bizard.", "a": "Thomas Ledl", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marie-Reine-du-Monde,_Montr%C3%A9al.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Salimiyya_Madrasa%2C_Damascus%2C_Syria_01.jpg/1280px-Salimiyya_Madrasa%2C_Damascus%2C_Syria_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": 33.5125, "lng": 36.29222222, "p": "Salimiyya Madrasa, Al-Qanawat Municipality, Syria", "h": "About this photo: Salimiyya Madrasa – Ottoman madrasa, part of the Sulaymaniyya Takiyya in Damascus, Syria\n\nSalimiyya Madrasa: The Salimiyya Madrasa is a 16th-century madrasa in Damascus, Syria. It is part of the Sulaymaniyya Takiyya, started under the Ottoman sultan Süleyman I. The madrasa was built after the rest of the complex, with stones which had been left over. It is possible that the madrasa was ordered by Süleyman himself right before his death in 1566 as it was called the \"Sulaymaniyya Madrasa\" in some sources upon completion, but over time it became known as the \"Salimiyya Madrasa\" .", "a": "Dickelbers", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salimiyya_Madrasa,_Damascus,_Syria_01.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/2011-06-22_11-53-59_South_Africa_-_Morningside.jpg/1280px-2011-06-22_11-53-59_South_Africa_-_Morningside.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": -29.84472222, "lng": 31.0225, "p": "Durban railway station, Durban, South Africa", "h": "About this photo: South Africa , Durban railway station from the top of Moses Mabhida Stadion\n\nDurban railway station: Durban railway station is the central railway station in the city of Durban, South Africa, located between Umgeni Road and NMR Avenue / Masabalala Yengwa Avenue just to the north of the central business district. It is the terminus of Shosholoza Meyl long-distance services from Johannesburg and Cape Town, and the hub of a network of Metrorail commuter rail services that stretch as far as KwaDukuza to the north, Kelso to the south, and Cato Ridge inland.\n\nThe wider area, Durban: Durban is the third-most populous city in South Africa, after Johannesburg and Cape Town, and the largest city in the province of KwaZulu-Natal. Situated on the east coast of South Africa, on the Natal Bay of the Indian Ocean, Durban is the busiest port city in sub-Saharan Africa and was formerly named Port Natal.", "a": "Hansueli Krapf This file was uploaded with Commonist .", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2011-06-22_11-53-59_South_Africa_-_Morningside.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Blanchs_teater_1964.jpg/1280px-Blanchs_teater_1964.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1964, "lat": 59.3325, "lng": 18.0699, "p": "Blanche Theatre, Stockholm, Sweden", "h": "About this photo: Blanchs teater vid Kungsträdgården i Stockholm, den 29 oktober 1964\n\nBlanche Theatre: Blanche Theatre or in Swedish Blancheteatern , was a private theater located in a building on West Tradgardsgatan next to Kungsträdgården in Stockholm, Sweden. The theater opened on March 31, 1879. It originally showed vaudeville but was changed to an arts venue around the mid-1880s. Under the leadership of Ernst Eklund 1917-1926 there was a successful blend of revues, classic dramas and debate. In its last years it was used as an alternative venue by the Swedish Royal Opera. The building was demolished in 1965 as part of a wholesale redevelopment of the Norrmalm district.\n\nThe wider area, Stockholm: Stockholm ɔlm] ) is the capital and most populous city of Sweden, as well as the largest urban area in the Nordic countries. Approximately 1 million people live in the municipality, with 1.6 million in the urban area, and 2.5 million in the metropolitan area. The city stretches across fourteen islands where Lake Mälaren flows into the Baltic Sea.", "a": "Unknown author Unknown author", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blanchs_teater_1964.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/2014_Moscow_Lenin_Museum.JPG/1280px-2014_Moscow_Lenin_Museum.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2014, "lat": 55.75611111, "lng": 37.61861111, "p": "Moscow City Hall, Moscow, Russia", "h": "About this photo: 2014 Moscow Lenin Museum Москва Центральный музей В. И. Ленина филиал Государственного исторического музея\n\nMoscow City Hall: The former Moscow City Hall is an ornate red-brick edifice situated immediately to the east of the State Historical Museum and notable in the history of architecture as a unique hybrid of the Russian Revival and Neo-Renaissance styles. During Soviet times it served as the Lenin Museum in Moscow.\n\nThe wider area, Moscow: Moscow is the capital and largest city of Russia, situated on the Moskva River in Central Russia. The city has a population estimated at more than 13 million residents within city limits, more than 19.1 million residents in the urban area, and more than 21.5 million residents in the metropolitan area.", "a": "Moscowjob.net", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014_Moscow_Lenin_Museum.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Bella_Vista%2C_Panama_City.jpg/1280px-Bella_Vista%2C_Panama_City.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2022, "lat": 8.9875, "lng": -79.5239, "p": "Bella Vista, Panama City, Bella Vista, Panama", "h": "Bella Vista, Panama City: Bella Vista is a corregimiento within Panama City, in Panama District, Panama Province, Panama with a population of 30,136 as of 2010. Its population as of 1990 was 24,986; its population as of 2000 was 28,421.\n\nThe wider area, Bella Vista: Bella Vista is the name of several places in the world:", "a": "LittleT889", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bella_Vista,_Panama_City.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Railway_Station_-_Phnom_Penh.JPG/1280px-Railway_Station_-_Phnom_Penh.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 11.5725, "lng": 104.9167, "p": "Phnom Penh railway station, Phnom Penh, Cambodia", "h": "Phnom Penh railway station: Phnom Penh railway station is a railway station in Phnom Penh, Cambodia. It is located next to the University of Health Sciences and the National University of Management as well as the Canadian embassy. This station was renovated and formally reopened October 22, 2010. Scheduled passenger train services between Phnom Penh and Sihanoukville resumed in May 2016 after having been suspended for 14 years. After that, many passenger train service continue to resume. As of May 2021, there is scheduled train service between Phnom Penh to Krong Pursat, Krong Battambang, Krong Sisophon, and Poipet. There will be a passenger train service from Phnom Penh to Bangkok by 2022. From April 2018 until 2020, trains operated by Royal Railway began running express from Phnom Penh International Airport to Phnom Penh railway station.\n\nThe wider area, Phnom Penh: Phnom Penh is the capital and most populous city of Cambodia. It has been the national capital since 1865 and has grown to become the nation's primate city and its political, economic, industrial, and cultural centre. The city's name derives from Wat Phnom, a Buddhist temple, and Lady Penh, the city's founder. It sits at the confluence of the Tonlé Sap and Mekong rivers, and is the start of the Bassac River.", "a": "Anilakeo", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Railway_Station_-_Phnom_Penh.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Nevskiy_86%282%29.JPG/1280px-Nevskiy_86%282%29.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2015, "lat": 59.9325, "lng": 30.35083333, "p": "Nevsky Prospect 86, Saint Petersburg, Russia", "h": "About this photo: 86 Nevsky Prospekt, St Petersburgh\n\nNevsky Prospect 86: Nevsky Prospect 86 is a neoclassical palace situated on the Nevsky Prospect in St Petersburg, Russia. It is also known as the Palace of Arts or the Palace of Zinaida Yusupova. The first palace on the site of the present palace was built in the middle of the 18th century, shortly after the founding of St Petersburg, by Prince Troubetzkoy. In the 1780s, his son, rebuilt and enlarged the original wooden structure in stone. At the end of the 18th century, the palace passed to the diplomat Prince Kurakin; it again changed hands during the first quarter of the 19th century, when it was sold to Colonel Fyodor Petrov-Solovovo. The new owner had the palace again rebuilt under the direction of the architect Mikhail Ovsyannikov. He created the neoclassical five columned centrepiece of the principal facade which is the corps de logis of the palace today.\n\nThe wider area, Saint Petersburg: Saint Petersburg, formerly known as Petrograd and then Leningrad, is the second-largest city in Russia, after Moscow, the nation's capital. St. Petersburg is situated on the Neva River at the head of the Gulf of Finland on the Baltic Sea. Its area of 1,439 km2 makes it the smallest administrative division of Russia.", "a": "User:Giano", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nevskiy_86(2).JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Chalmers-Wesley_United_Church%2C_Quebec_City.jpg/1280px-Chalmers-Wesley_United_Church%2C_Quebec_City.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 46.81006, "lng": -71.20901, "p": "Chalmers-Wesley United Church, Quebec, Canada", "h": "About this photo: Chalmers-Wesley United Church, Quebec City\n\nChalmers-Wesley United Church: Chalmers-Wesley United Church is a Protestant church located within the walls of Old Quebec at 78, rue Sainte-Ursule in Quebec City, Quebec, Canada. Designed by architect John Wells for the Free Presbyterian Church of Canada, the church was built from 1851-1853 and opened for worship on March 6, 1853. It is particularly admired for its Gothic Revival design. In 1925, the church became part of the newly formed United Church of Canada. Composer William Reed was notably the church's organist from 1900-1913. In 1931, the congregation of the nearby Wesleyan Methodist Church, which had also become part of The United Church of Canada, merged with Chalmers United Church and was renamed Chalmers-Wesley United Church.\n\nThe wider area, Quebec: Quebec is Canada's largest province by area. Located in Central Canada, it is the country's only Francophone-majority province. It is home to speakers of Québécois French. It shares borders with Ontario to the west, Newfoundland and Labrador to the northeast, New Brunswick to the southeast and a coastal border with the territory of Nunavut. To the south, it borders the United States.", "a": "Wilfredor", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chalmers-Wesley_United_Church,_Quebec_City.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Bausch%C3%A4nzli_-_Stadthausquai_-_Limmat_-_Grossm%C3%BCnster_-_Limmatquai_-_Quaibr%C3%BCcke_2012-07-30_09-01-26.JPG/1280px-Bausch%C3%A4nzli_-_Stadthausquai_-_Limmat_-_Grossm%C3%BCnster_-_Limmatquai_-_Quaibr%C3%BCcke_2012-07-30_09-01-26.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 47.36768056, "lng": 8.54240278, "p": "Bauschänzli, Zurich, Switzerland", "h": "About this photo: View from Quaibrücke in Zürich : Bauschänzli in the Limmat river, Stadthausquai to the left, and Grossmünster and Limmatquai to the right.\n\nBauschänzli: Bauschänzli is an artificial island, town square, and public park in Zürich, Switzerland. Bauschänzli is one of the last remains of the Baroque fortifications of Zürich which began in 1642. The neighboring Schanzengraben and the Old Botanical Garden are part of these remains. Since 1907, the island has been used as a restaurant, although it is officially a public square and park.\n\nThe wider area, Zurich: Zurich is the largest city in Switzerland and the capital of the canton of Zurich. It is in north-central Switzerland, at the northwestern tip of Lake Zurich. As of the end of 2025, the municipality had a population of 440,935, while the urban area was home to 1.45 million people , and the Zurich metropolitan area had a total population of 2.1 million . Zurich is a hub for railways, roads, and air traffic.", "a": "Roland zh", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bausch%C3%A4nzli_-_Stadthausquai_-_Limmat_-_Grossm%C3%BCnster_-_Limmatquai_-_Quaibr%C3%BCcke_2012-07-30_09-01-26.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Nelson_Street_2018.jpg/1280px-Nelson_Street_2018.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 22.31877778, "lng": 114.17196667, "p": "Nelson Street, Hong Kong, China", "h": "Nelson Street: Nelson Street is a street in Mong Kok, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China. The street is 240 metres in length and runs in the east–west direction. It can be accessed from exits E1 and E2 of the Mong Kok station. The section between Portland and Sai Yeung Choi South Street is closed to vehicular traffic.\n\nThe wider area, Hong Kong: Hong Kong is a special administrative region of China. Situated on China's southern coast just south of Shenzhen, it consists of Hong Kong Island, Kowloon, and the New Territories. With 7.5 million residents in a 1,114-square-kilometre territory, Hong Kong is the fourth-most densely populated region in the world.", "a": "Wpcpey", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nelson_Street_2018.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Village_of_Phyang.jpg/1280px-Village_of_Phyang.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 34.189399, "lng": 77.489076, "p": "Phyang, Leh, India", "h": "About this photo: Village of Phyang, Ladakh, India\n\nPhyang: Phyang is a village and tehsil in Leh district of Ladakh in India. The Phyang Monastery is in this village. This is one of the largest inhabited villages of Ladakh, comprising eight clusters: Phulungs, Phyang, Tsakma, Changmachan, Gaon, Thangnak, Chusgo and Mankhang. It is in a south-facing valley in the Ladakh Range of mountains. The village is about 15 km long and 1.5 km average width. There is an old dilapidated castle near the cluster of Phyang on a hilltop. Just below this castle, there is a single-room temple, Lobon, with paintings of Ladakh, but in the tourism industry, it is known as Guru Lhakhang. The Tokpo river is in the middle of the valley. The source of the river is small glaciers in upper Spangkul and Lungnak valley northwest of the village at an altitude of about 5,200 m . The river joins the Indus River near Phey village at a lower altitude of 3,250 m .\n\nThe wider area, Leh: Leh is a city in Indian-administered Ladakh in the disputed Kashmir region. It is the largest city and the joint capital of Ladakh. Leh, located in the Leh district, has been the capital of Ladakh since the medieval period and was historically capital of the Kingdom of Ladakh.", "a": "Bernard Gagnon Canon EOS REBEL T4i", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Village_of_Phyang.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Meydan-e_Azadi_Metro_Station_entrance.jpg/1280px-Meydan-e_Azadi_Metro_Station_entrance.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2021, "lat": 35.7008, "lng": 51.3785, "p": "Meydan-e Azadi Metro Station, Tehran, Iran", "h": "About this photo: ورودی ایستگاه متروی میدان آزادی - اسفند ۱۳۹۹\n\nMeydan-e Azadi Metro Station: Meydan-e Azadi Metro Station is the sixteenth station of the Tehran Metro Line 4 and is located in Azadi Square, Shaheed Baradaran Rahmani Highway, between Ostad Moein and before Bimeh.\n\nThe wider area, Tehran: Tehran is the capital and largest city of Iran. It is also the capital of Tehran province and the administrative center for Tehran County and its Central District.", "a": "Kasir", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meydan-e_Azadi_Metro_Station_entrance.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Armagh_St_Bridge%2C_2009.jpg/1280px-Armagh_St_Bridge%2C_2009.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": -43.52877, "lng": 172.63475, "p": "Armagh Street Bridge, Christchurch, New Zealand", "h": "About this photo: The Armagh Street Bridge in Christchurch in 2009.\n\nArmagh Street Bridge: The Armagh Street Bridge is a historic bridge on Armagh Street in Christchurch Central City. It sits between the Convention Centre Precinct and the Magistrate's Court at the south-west corner of Victoria Square. Its initial importance was it making a connection to the Canterbury Provincial Council Buildings just beyond the Magistrate's Court.\n\nThe wider area, Christchurch: Christchurch , also known by its Māori name Ōtautahi, is the largest city in the South Island and the second-largest city by urban area population in New Zealand. Christchurch has an urban population of 407,800, and a metropolitan population of 556,500. It is located in the Canterbury Region, near the centre of the east coast of the South Island, east of the Canterbury Plains.", "a": "Paul & Rebecca", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armagh_St_Bridge,_2009.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Entr%C3%A9e_Gare_Ch%C3%A2telet_Halles_place_Marguerite_Navarre_Paris_2.jpg/1280px-Entr%C3%A9e_Gare_Ch%C3%A2telet_Halles_place_Marguerite_Navarre_Paris_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 48.8615, "lng": 2.3465, "p": "Châtelet–Les Halles station, Paris, France", "h": "About this photo: Entrée dans la gare Châtelet - Les Halles, place Marguerite-de-Navarre, Paris.\n\nChâtelet–Les Halles station: Châtelet–Les Halles station is a major train hub in Paris and the largest underground station in the world. Opened in 1977, it is the central transit hub for the Paris metropolitan area, connecting three of five RER commuter-rail lines and five of sixteen Métro lines. The hub hosts 750,000 travellers per weekday and platforms separated by up to 800 metres . It is named after the nearby Place du Châtelet public square and Les Halles, the former wholesale food market of Paris, now a shopping mall.\n\nThe wider area, Paris: Paris is the capital and largest city of France, with an estimated city population of 2.04 million in an area of 105.4 km2 , as of January 2026, and a metropolitan population of 13.3 million . Located on the Seine in the centre of the Île-de-France region, it is the largest metropolitan area and fourth-most populous city in the European Union .", "a": "Chabe01", "l": "CC BY 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entr%C3%A9e_Gare_Ch%C3%A2telet_Halles_place_Marguerite_Navarre_Paris_2.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Tulsi_Ghat.jpg/1280px-Tulsi_Ghat.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 25.28983333, "lng": 83.00650972, "p": "Tulsi Ghat, Varanasi, India", "h": "About this photo: Tulsi ghat is one of the ghats in Varanasi.\n\nTulsi Ghat: Tulsi Ghat is one of the ghats in Varanasi, in India. It is named after poet Tulsidas who lived there while he wrote the Ramcharitmanas and Hanuman Chalisa. Earlier, Tulsi Ghat was known as Lolark Ghat. It was in the year 1941 that Tulsi Ghat was made pucca by industrialist, Baldeo Das Birla. Tulsi Ghat is accessible by boat, and it is possible to hire a private boat tour on the banks of river Ganga in Varanasi that allows to visit all important ghats and watch the sunrise.\n\nThe wider area, Varanasi: Varanasi , or Kashi, is a city on the Ganges river in northern India that has a central place in the traditions of pilgrimage, death, and mourning in the Hindu world. The city also has a syncretic tradition of Islamic artisanship that underpins its religious tourism. Located in the middle-Ganges valley in the southeastern part of the state of Uttar Pradesh, Varanasi lies on the left bank of the river.", "a": "Nandanupadhyay", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tulsi_Ghat.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Ponte_degli_Scalzi_-_Venice_-_2016_%282%29.jpg/1280px-Ponte_degli_Scalzi_-_Venice_-_2016_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2016, "lat": 45.44111111, "lng": 12.3225, "p": "Ponte degli Scalzi, Venice, Italy", "h": "Ponte degli Scalzi: The Ponte degli Scalzi , is one of only four bridges in Venice to span the Grand Canal. The bridge connects the sestieri of Santa Croce and Cannaregio. On the north side, Cannaregio, are the Chiesa degli Scalzi and the Santa Lucia railway station. The south side is the sestiere of Santa Croce. Designed by Eugenio Miozzi, it was completed in 1934, replacing an Austrian iron bridge. It is a stone arch bridge. Ponte degli Scalzi is located close to the site of construction of the fourth bridge over the Grand Canal, popularly known as Ponte di Calatrava although it was formally inaugurated as the Ponte della Costituzione. Construction was delayed in part due to controversy over its modern style, but the basic span was finally in place on August 11, 2007, and the bridge was opened for public use on September 11, 2008. This bridge is closer to the bus station than the Scalzi bridge.\n\nThe wider area, Venice: Venice is a coastal city in northeastern Italy and the capital of the region of Veneto, as well as the Metropolitan City of Venice. It is built on a group of 126 islands that are separated by expanses of open water and by canals; portions of the city are linked by 472 bridges. The islands are in the shallow Venetian Lagoon, an enclosed bay lying between the mouths of the Po and the Piave rivers .", "a": "José Luiz", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ponte_degli_Scalzi_-_Venice_-_2016_(2).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Gebroeders_Douwes%2C_Afb_B00000031514.jpg/1280px-Gebroeders_Douwes%2C_Afb_B00000031514.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1867, "lat": 52.36734, "lng": 4.902323, "p": "Leprozengracht, Amsterdam, Netherlands", "h": "About this photo: Beschrijving De Leprozengracht gezien naar de Mozes en Aäronkerk Na de demping in 1882 werd dit het Waterlooplein. Oorspronkelijke kabinetfoto uitgegeven door A. Jager. Documenttype foto Vervaardiger Gebroeders Douwes Collectie Collectie Kunsthandel Gebr.\n\nLeprozengracht: The Leprozengracht was a canal in Amsterdam that defined one side of the Vlooienburg island. Leprozengracht and the connected Houtgracht canal were filled in 1882 to form the Waterlooplein.\n\nThe wider area, Amsterdam: Amsterdam is the capital and largest city of the Netherlands. As of 1 January 2026, it had a population of 941,873 within the city proper, 1,457,018 in the urban area and 2,480,394 in the metropolitan area. Located in the Dutch province of North Holland, Amsterdam is colloquially referred to as the \"Venice of the North\", for its large number of canals, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.", "a": "Gebroeders Douwes (uitgever) (1866-1900)", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebroeders_Douwes,_Afb_B00000031514.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Brazil-00988_-_Rio_de_Janeiro_Municipal_Theatre_%2848984301008%29.jpg/1280px-Brazil-00988_-_Rio_de_Janeiro_Municipal_Theatre_%2848984301008%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": -22.90922222, "lng": -43.17649167, "p": "Theatro Municipal (Rio de Janeiro), Rio de Janeiro, Brazil", "h": "About this photo: The Rio de Janeiro Municipal Theatre, is the leading playhouse in Brazil and one of the most important in South America. Opened in 1909, the theatre receives the major international and Brazilian artists, from dance, music and opera.\n\nTheatro Municipal (Rio de Janeiro): The Theatro Municipal is an opera house in the Centro district of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. Built in the early twentieth century, it is considered to be one of the most beautiful and important theaters in the country. The building is designed in an eclectic style, inspired by the Paris Opéra of Charles Garnier. The outside walls are inscribed with the names of classic European and Brazilian artists. It is located near the National Library and the National Fine Arts Museum, overlooking the spacious Cinelândia square.\n\nThe wider area, Rio de Janeiro: Rio de Janeiro, also known simply as Rio, is the capital and largest city of the state of Rio de Janeiro. It is the second-largest city in Brazil after São Paulo, and the largest by number of international tourists. It has a population of 6 million in the city proper, and 13 million in its metropolitan area as of 2025. It is also the sixth-most-populous city in the Americas.", "a": "Dennis G. Jarvis", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brazil-00988_-_Rio_de_Janeiro_Municipal_Theatre_(48984301008).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/%D0%95%D0%B2%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%89%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%8C_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2_2012_01.JPG/1280px-%D0%95%D0%B2%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%89%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%8C_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2_2012_01.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 50.4516, "lng": 30.5275, "p": "Hotel Dnipro, Kyiv, Ukraine", "h": "About this photo: Європейська площа у Києві , вигляд у бік вулиці Михайла Грушевського\n\nHotel Dnipro: Hotel Dnipro is a four-star hotel located in central Kyiv, the capital of Ukraine, on European Square, next to Khreschatyk Street and near Maidan Nezalezhnosti . The hotel was built in 1964 in a location which originally was occupied by Kyiv's Hotel Yevropa. The interior and design of the restaurant in the Hotel Dnipro was made by the architect Irma Karakis. Formally state owned, in July 2020 the hotel was sold to the private real estate company Smartland. Smartland won an open auction with their bid of ₴1,111,111,222.22 ).\n\nThe wider area, Kyiv: Kyiv, also spelt Kiev, is the capital and most populous city of Ukraine. Located in the north-central part of the country, it straddles both banks of the Dnieper River. As of January 2022, the population of Kyiv was 2,952,301, making it the seventh-most populous city in Europe. Kyiv is an important industrial, scientific, educational, and cultural center.", "a": "AMY", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%95%D0%B2%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%89%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%8C_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2_2012_01.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/De_Brouck%C3%A8replein_Brussel_juli_2021.jpg/1280px-De_Brouck%C3%A8replein_Brussel_juli_2021.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2021, "lat": 50.85138889, "lng": 4.3525, "p": "Place de Brouckère, Brussels, Belgium", "h": "About this photo: De Brouckèreplein Brussel juli 2021\n\nPlace de Brouckère: The Place de Brouckère or De Brouckèreplein ˌplɛin]) is a major square in central Brussels, Belgium. It was created following the covering of the river Senne , replacing the Temple of the Augustinians, which was demolished in 1893. It is named in honour of Charles de Brouckère, a former mayor of the City of Brussels and professor at the Free University of Brussels, who played an important political role during the Belgian Revolution of 1830. The square measures approximately 50 by 350 metres and is nearly entirely paved. The Place de Brouckère is located in the perspective of the Boulevard Émile Jacqmain/Émile Jacqmainlaan and the Boulevard Adolphe Max/Adolphe Maxlaan to the north, forming a \"Y\" crossroad, and the Boulevard Anspach/Anspachlaan to the south.\n\nThe wider area, Brussels: Brussels, officially the Brussels-Capital Region, is a region of Belgium comprising 19 municipalities, including the City of Brussels, which is the capital of Belgium. The Brussels-Capital Region is located in the central portion of the country.", "a": "Riki", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Brouck%C3%A8replein_Brussel_juli_2021.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Suomenlinna_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Suomenlinna_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2005, "lat": 60.17083333, "lng": 24.9375, "p": "Helsinki, Finland", "h": "About this photo: Suomenlinna , a fortress near Helsinki , Finland . Picture taken from a ferry.\n\nHelsinki: Helsinki, until about 1930 known in English by its Swedish name of Helsingfors, is the capital and most populous city in Finland. It is on the shore of the Gulf of Finland and is the seat of southern Finland's Uusimaa region. About 694,000 people live in the municipality, with 1.3 million in the capital region and 1.6 million in the metropolitan area. As the most populous urban area in Finland, it is the country's most significant centre for politics, education, finance, culture, and research. Helsinki is 80 kilometres north of Tallinn, Estonia, 400 kilometres east of Stockholm, Sweden, and 300 kilometres west of Saint Petersburg, Russia. Helsinki is a bilingual municipality with Finnish and Swedish as its official languages.", "a": "Michal Pise, Michal.Pise", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suomenlinna_(cropped).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Sukiennice_and_Main_Market_Square_Krakow_Poland.JPG/1280px-Sukiennice_and_Main_Market_Square_Krakow_Poland.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 50.06166667, "lng": 19.93722222, "p": "Main Square, Kraków, Kraków, Poland", "h": "About this photo: Sukiennice and Main Market Square as seen from St. Mary's Basilica, Krakow, Poland\n\nMain Square, Kraków: The Main Market Square is a historic square in the Old Town of Kraków, Poland. It is the principal urban space at the centre of the city. It dates back to the 13th century and, with an area of 4 ha , it is sometimes called one of the largest medieval town squares in Europe. The Main Market Square is a square space surrounded by historic tenement houses and churches. The centre of the square is dominated by the Cloth Hall , rebuilt in 1555 in the Renaissance style, topped by a attic or Polish parapet decorated with carved masks. On one side of the cloth hall is the Town Hall Tower , on the other the 11th century Church of St. Adalbert and 1898 Adam Mickiewicz Monument. Rising above the square are the Gothic towers of St. Mary's Basilica . Kraków Main Square does not have a town hall, because it has not survived to the present day.\n\nThe wider area, Kraków: Kraków, officially the Royal Capital City of Kraków, is the second-largest and one of the oldest cities in Poland. Situated on the Vistula river in Lesser Poland Voivodeship, the city has a population of 816,614 , with 1,428,363 people living in the Kraków metropolitan area.", "a": "Jorge Lascar", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sukiennice_and_Main_Market_Square_Krakow_Poland.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/ShrineofAmirTimur.jpg/1280px-ShrineofAmirTimur.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2016, "lat": 39.64833333, "lng": 66.96888889, "p": "Gur-e-Amir, Samarkand City, Uzbekistan", "h": "About this photo: The shrine of Tamerlane in Samarkand , Uzbekistan\n\nGur-e-Amir: The Gur-e-Amir, also transcribed Gūr-i Amīr or Guri Amir is a mausoleum of the Turco-Mongol conqueror Timur in Samarkand, Uzbekistan. It occupies an important place in the history of architecture of Turkestan as the precursor for and having influence on the later Mughal tombs, including the Bagh-e-Babur in Kabul, Humayun's Tomb in Delhi and the Taj Mahal in Agra, built by Timur's descendants, the Mughals, that shaped Indian culture with Central Asian influences. The mausoleum has been heavily restored over the course of its existence.", "a": "Willard84", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ShrineofAmirTimur.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Fiolstr%C3%A6de_18.jpg/1280px-Fiolstr%C3%A6de_18.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2008, "lat": 55.68068889, "lng": 12.5731, "p": "Fiolstræde 18, Copenhagen, Denmark", "h": "About this photo: The listed Building at 18 Fiolstræde seen from the roof of Hotel Sankt Petri in Central Copenhagen, Denmark\n\nFiolstræde 18: Fiolstræde 18 is a half-timbered building situated at the corner of Fiolstræde and Krystalgade in the Old Town of Copenhagen, Denmark. It was constructed in 1734 as part of the rebuilding of the city following the Copenhagen Fire of 1728. It was listed in the Danish registry of protected buildings and places in 1939.\n\nThe wider area, Copenhagen: Copenhagen is the capital and most populous city of Denmark, with an urban population of 1.4 million. The city is situated mainly on the island of Zealand , with a smaller part on the island of Amager. Copenhagen is separated from Malmö, Sweden, by the Øresund strait. The Øresund Bridge connects the two cities by rail and road.", "a": "Jacob Bøtter from Copenhagen, Denmark", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fiolstr%C3%A6de_18.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/66-183_Kabul_Khyber_Restaurant_Afghanistan_1966.jpg/1280px-66-183_Kabul_Khyber_Restaurant_Afghanistan_1966.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1966, "lat": 34.520063, "lng": 69.180688, "p": "Ministry of Finance (Afghanistan), Kabul, Afghanistan", "h": "About this photo: 66-183 Kabul Khyber Restaurant Afghanistan 1966\n\nMinistry of Finance (Afghanistan): The Ministry of Finance of Afghanistan is responsible for the implementation and execution of the budget, collection of taxes, organization, and control of public expenses in Afghanistan; it also controls the management of the Custom Affairs. It is located next to the Pashtunistan Square in the Wazir Akbar Khan neighborhood of Kabul, Afghanistan. The Ministry of Finance provides a quarterly report to inform the public and the executive cabinet of advancements in Afghanistan's financial sector. The current Finance Minister is Nasir Akhund.\n\nThe wider area, Kabul: Kabul is the capital and largest city of Afghanistan. It is within the jurisdiction of Kabul District and has an estimated population of 5,333,284 people. Located in the eastern half of the country, forming part of the Kabul Province, the city is administratively divided into five zones and 22 municipal districts. The current mayor of the city is Mawlawi Abdul Rashid.", "a": "wilford peloquin", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:66-183_Kabul_Khyber_Restaurant_Afghanistan_1966.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Palais_Caprara-Geymueller_panoramic.jpg/1280px-Palais_Caprara-Geymueller_panoramic.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": 48.21027778, "lng": 16.36583333, "p": "Palais Caprara-Geymüller, Vienna, Austria", "h": "About this photo: Palais Caprara-Geymüller This media shows the protected monument with ObjektID 40908 in Austria.\n\nPalais Caprara-Geymüller: Palais Caprara-Geymüller, sometimes known as Palais Caprara, is a Baroque palace in Vienna, Austria. The Vienna Stock Exchange is situated in the palace.\n\nThe wider area, Vienna: Vienna is the capital, most populous city, and one of the nine states of Austria. It is Austria's primate city, with just over two million inhabitants. Its larger metropolitan area has a population of nearly 2.9 million, representing nearly one-third of the country's population.", "a": "Palais_Caprara-Geymueller_004.JPG : Gryffindor Palais_Caprara-Geymueller_003.JPG", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palais_Caprara-Geymueller_panoramic.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/LATimesBuilding.jpg/1280px-LATimesBuilding.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2008, "lat": 34.053009, "lng": -118.244596, "p": "Times Mirror Square, Los Angeles, United States", "h": "About this photo: Los Angeles Times Building — in Downtown Los Angeles, California. The Art Deco building was designed by Gordon Kaufmann .\n\nTimes Mirror Square: Times Mirror Square is a complex of buildings on the block bounded by Spring, Broadway, First and Second streets in the Civic Center district of Downtown Los Angeles. It was headquarters of the Los Angeles Times until 2018. It is currently vacant, with plans being proposed regarding how to best utilize the existing buildings and the total ground area of the site.\n\nThe wider area, Los Angeles: Los Angeles is the most populous city in the U.S. state of California, and the commercial, financial, and cultural center of Southern California. With an estimated 3.87 million residents within the city limits as of 2025, it is the second-most populous city in the United States, behind New York City, and the largest city in the Western United States.", "a": "Minnaert", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LATimesBuilding.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Denver_Central_Library._View_from_south-east.jpg/1280px-Denver_Central_Library._View_from_south-east.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 39.737313, "lng": -104.988246, "p": "Denver Public Library, Denver, United States", "h": "About this photo: Denver Central Library. View from south-east.\n\nDenver Public Library: Denver Public Library is the public library system of the City and County of Denver, Colorado, United States. The system includes Denver Central Library, located in the Golden Triangle district of Downtown Denver, as well as 28 branch locations and two bookmobiles. The library's collection totals more than 2 million items, including books, reference materials, movies, music, and photographs. Of that total, more than 347,000 items are in specific collections including the Special Collections and Archives, Blair-Caldwell African American Research Library, and Reference Department holdings.\n\nThe wider area, Denver: Denver , officially the City and County of Denver, is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Colorado. Officially a consolidated city and county, it is located in the South Platte River valley on the western edge of the High Plains, and is just east of the Front Range of the Rocky Mountains .", "a": "Sarbjit Bahga", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denver_Central_Library._View_from_south-east.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Adelaide_Type_C_tram_179_in_Currie_St_at_King_William_St_16_Oct_1953.jpg/1280px-Adelaide_Type_C_tram_179_in_Currie_St_at_King_William_St_16_Oct_1953.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1953, "lat": -34.92455, "lng": 138.5985, "p": "Adelaide, Australia", "h": "About this photo: An Adelaide Type C tram, number 179, in Currie Street at the intersection with King William Street on 16 October 1953\n\nThe wider area, Adelaide: Adelaide is the capital and most populous city of South Australia, as well as the fifth-most populous city in Australia. The name \"Adelaide\" may refer to either Greater Adelaide or the Adelaide central business district; the demonym Adelaidean is used to denote the city and the residents of Adelaide.", "a": "Douglas A. Colquhoun", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adelaide_Type_C_tram_179_in_Currie_St_at_King_William_St_16_Oct_1953.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/MTT_Type_F_tram_no._227_at_Peacock_Road_near_South_Terrace%2C_Adelaide%2C_9_June_1956.jpg/1280px-MTT_Type_F_tram_no._227_at_Peacock_Road_near_South_Terrace%2C_Adelaide%2C_9_June_1956.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1956, "lat": -34.9357, "lng": 138.6004, "p": "Adelaide, Australia", "h": "About this photo: A Type F drop-centre tram of Adelaide's Municipal Tramways Trust is heading south down Peacock Road, just having crossed over South Terrace. Its destination is the Colonel Light Gardens terminus on Goodwood Road.\n\nThe wider area, Adelaide: Adelaide is the capital and most populous city of South Australia, as well as the fifth-most populous city in Australia. The name \"Adelaide\" may refer to either Greater Adelaide or the Adelaide central business district; the demonym Adelaidean is used to denote the city and the residents of Adelaide.", "a": "John C. Radcliffe", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MTT_Type_F_tram_no._227_at_Peacock_Road_near_South_Terrace,_Adelaide,_9_June_1956.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Former_Osaka_Education_Life_Insurance_Building_Osaka_Japan_1998_1_26_002.jpg/1280px-Former_Osaka_Education_Life_Insurance_Building_Osaka_Japan_1998_1_26_002.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1998, "lat": 34.689694, "lng": 135.503806, "p": "Osaka, Japan", "h": "About this photo: 大阪市にある、旧大阪教育生命保険のビル。辰野金吾設計。\n\nThe wider area, Osaka: Osaka , ó̞ó̞-] ) is a designated city in the Kansai region of Honshu in Japan. It is the capital of and most populous city in Osaka Prefecture, and the third-most populous city proper in Japan, following the special wards of Tokyo and Yokohama.", "a": "Mr.Naka guutarabunko", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Former_Osaka_Education_Life_Insurance_Building_Osaka_Japan_1998_1_26_002.jpg"}, {"u": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Hindustan_Building%2C_Calcutta_in_1945.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled", "y": 1945, "lat": 22.567181, "lng": 88.354476, "p": "Kolkata, India", "h": "About this photo: Original description: \" Hindusthan building, one of the most modern in Calcutta, was built for an insurance company but occupied upon its completion by the U.S. Army.\n\nThe wider area, Kolkata: Kolkata, also known as Calcutta , is the capital and largest city of the Indian state of West Bengal. It lies on the eastern bank of the Hooghly River, 80 km west of the border with Bangladesh. It is the primary financial and commercial centre of eastern India and one of the gateways to northeastern India.", "a": "Clyde Waddell", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hindustan_Building,_Calcutta_in_1945.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Naftaproduktionsbolaget_Br%C3%B6derna_Nobel%2C_Baku_%285889748933%29.jpg/1280px-Naftaproduktionsbolaget_Br%C3%B6derna_Nobel%2C_Baku_%285889748933%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1903, "lat": 40.392711, "lng": 49.84806, "p": "Baku, Azerbaijan", "h": "About this photo: SKC892 Skioptikonbild med motiv från professorn och kemisten A.W. Cronquists besök i Baku- oljedistrikt 1903. Naftaproduktionsbolaget Bröderna Nobels anläggningar i Baku, Azerbaijan. The Nobel Brothers Naftaproductioncorporation in Baku, Azerbaijan. Photo: A.W. Cronquist, 1903.\n\nThe wider area, Baku: Baku is the capital and largest city of Azerbaijan, as well as the largest city on the Caspian Sea and in the Caucasus region. Baku is 28 metres below sea level, which makes it the lowest lying national capital in the world and the largest city in the world below sea level. Baku lies on the southern shore of the Absheron Peninsula, along the Bay of Baku.", "a": "Tekniska museet", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naftaproduktionsbolaget_Br%C3%B6derna_Nobel,_Baku_(5889748933).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/La_Paz%2C_Bolivia1.jpg/1280px-La_Paz%2C_Bolivia1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1999, "lat": -16.495019, "lng": -68.148794, "p": "La Paz, Bolivia", "h": "About this photo: The view from Hotel Italia. Avenida Manco Kapac 303, La Paz, Bolivia, 591-2-322177.\n\nThe wider area, La Paz: La Paz , officially Nuestra Señora de La Paz , is the seat of government of Bolivia. With 755,732 residents as of 2024, it is the third-most populous city in Bolivia. Its metropolitan area, which includes the neighboring city of El Alto, and other smaller towns, is the second most populous urban area in Bolivia, with a population of 2.2 million, after Santa Cruz de la Sierra with a population of 2.3 million.", "a": "wallygrom", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Paz,_Bolivia1.jpg"}];
